// Details for the columnar database plate, part 1: operations, access control, connections, planning.
ATLAS.details("columnar-database", {
  "workload-queues": {
    s: "Assigns each query to a class with its own concurrency cap, memory share and priority, so interactive dashboards stay responsive while heavy reports and loads run alongside.",
    d: "Each incoming query is matched by user, role, tag or estimated cost to a queue. A queue limits how many queries run at once, how much memory and how many threads they may take in total, and what happens to the overflow: wait, queue with a timeout, or reject. Priorities let short queries overtake long ones, and some systems also move a query to a slower class once it has used too much time. The hard part is that cost is only known after the fact: an estimate that admits a query that turns out to be a hundred times larger damages everyone in its class.",
    k: ["Admission by estimated cost vs admission by user class: estimates adapt to the actual query but are often wrong; classes are predictable but treat a trivial and a huge query from one user the same.", "Hard memory shares per queue vs shared pool with borrowing: hard shares isolate tenants but leave memory idle; borrowing uses it fully but a burst can evict someone else's query."],
    p: ["One queue for everything lets a single nightly report drive dashboard latency from milliseconds to minutes.", "Queued queries with no timeout pile up behind a stuck query until clients reconnect and add even more load."],
    v: "Start ten slow reports in the batch queue, then run a small dashboard query in the interactive queue and confirm it finishes within its normal latency while the system table shows the reports waiting or capped.",
    dep: ["query-cost-limits", "memory-accounting", "system-metrics-tables"]
  },
  "query-cost-limits": {
    s: "Per-query ceilings on rows or bytes read, memory used, execution time, result size and thread count, enforced during execution so a mistake cannot take down the cluster.",
    d: "Limits are checked as the query runs, not only before it starts. Scan operators count rows and bytes read, operators report memory to the accounting layer, and a timer watches elapsed time. On breach the query is stopped with an error or, for result size, truncated. Limits can be set per profile, per user or per query. The difficulty is placing them: too low and legitimate reports fail, too high and they protect nothing. Distributed queries must sum usage across nodes, and some limits are enforced only per node, so the cluster total can be many times the stated limit.",
    k: ["Fail on breach vs return a partial result: failing is unambiguous and cheap to reason about; partial answers keep dashboards alive but can silently mislead.", "Estimated limits before start vs measured limits during run: estimates refuse obviously bad queries immediately, while measured limits are exact but only fire after resources are already spent."],
    p: ["A per-node memory limit is read as a per-query limit, and a query on a hundred nodes consumes a hundred times more than intended.", "A row-read limit stops a query only after it has already scanned most of a table, so the protection comes too late."],
    v: "Set a limit of one billion rows read, run a full scan of a larger table, and confirm the query aborts with a limit error close to that count, with the abort visible in the query log and no leftover memory held.",
    dep: ["memory-accounting", "configuration-profiles", "scan-planning"]
  },
  "query-profiler": {
    s: "Records where each query spent time and bytes: per-operator timings, rows in and out, granules skipped, and CPU stack samples, so a slow query can be explained after it ran.",
    d: "Every operator counts the rows, bytes and wall time it handled, and the scan counts how many blocks the index let it skip versus read. A sampling profiler can also capture CPU stacks across all query threads at a fixed interval. The executed plan with these counters is stored with the query log entry or shown on demand. The trade-off is overhead and fidelity: counting is cheap, tracing every operator call is not, and with parallel pipelines time is summed across threads, so operator times can add up to more than the wall clock.",
    k: ["Always-on counters vs on-demand tracing: counters cost almost nothing and exist for every past query, but tracing gives detail only for queries you thought to trace in advance.", "Sampled stacks vs exact instrumentation: sampling finds hot code without slowing queries, but misses short bursts and cannot say how many times something was called."],
    p: ["Reading summed thread time as elapsed time makes a well-parallelized scan look slower than the query actually was.", "Profiling only the final plan hides that most of the time went to planning or waiting in a queue."],
    v: "Run a query with a selective filter and confirm the profile shows the scan reading a small share of granules, the filter operator's rows-in far above rows-out, and per-operator times that account for the measured latency.",
    dep: ["system-metrics-tables", "pipelined-operators"]
  },
  "system-metrics-tables": {
    s: "Queryable tables for the finished query log, running queries, parts, merges, replication queues, cache hit rates and per-node counters, so the engine monitors itself with its own SQL.",
    d: "The server writes its own telemetry into internal tables: one row per finished query, one per data part, one per merge, and rolling counters for memory, I/O and network. Current state such as running queries and the merge queue is exposed as live views. Cluster-wide variants fan out to every node. Because the log tables are ordinary columnar tables, they are cheap to fill and quick to aggregate. The difficulty is volume and lag: the query log for a busy system grows quickly, is flushed in batches, and a node that crashes may lose its last few seconds of rows.",
    k: ["Log tables written by the engine itself vs an external collector: no extra system to run and full SQL access, but the same cluster that is failing must be asked about its failure.", "Per-query rows vs sampled rows: one row each makes any query traceable but grows without bound; sampling keeps the table small but loses the one query an operator needs."],
    p: ["The log table grows until it competes for disk with user data because no retention rule was set on it.", "Per-node tables are read from one node and so describe only that node's share of a distributed query."],
    v: "Run a query tagged with a known comment, then select it from the cluster-wide query log and confirm rows read, bytes, duration and memory peak are present from every participating node within the flush interval.",
    dep: ["retention-ttl", "insert-pipeline"]
  },
  "retention-ttl": {
    s: "Rules that expire or move data automatically when it reaches a given age, by dropping whole partitions or rewriting parts during merges, so tables of events do not grow forever.",
    d: "A rule is an expression on a time column, such as older than ninety days: delete, move to a cheaper tier, or aggregate to a coarser grain. When partitions line up with the rule, expiry is dropping whole files, which is instant and free. When they do not, the merge process rewrites parts and omits expired rows, which costs real I/O and happens only when merges run. Each part stores the minimum and maximum of the rule's expression so most parts can be judged without being read. The pitfall is silent lag: expiry is eventual, and an expired row may remain readable for a while.",
    k: ["Partition-aligned expiry vs row-level expiry: dropping whole partitions is nearly free but needs the partition key to match the rule; row-level expiry fits any rule but rewrites data.", "Delete vs move to a cheaper tier: deletion frees the space but cannot be undone; moving keeps history queryable at lower cost but slower reads."],
    p: ["Queries assume rows past their retention date are gone, but expiry has not yet run, producing counts that differ between runs.", "Row-level expiry on a huge table triggers rewrites of old parts that merges had left alone and saturates disk for hours."],
    v: "Create a table with a one-day rule, insert rows dated three days ago, run the expiry and confirm they vanish from queries, the system part table shows the parts dropped or rewritten, and storage used decreases.",
    dep: ["background-merges", "partition-pruning", "tiered-storage"]
  },
  "configuration-profiles": {
    s: "Named bundles of settings and limits applied to users or roles, with a precedence order, so each workload gets suitable thread counts, memory and optimizer behavior without code changes.",
    d: "A profile lists settings such as maximum threads, memory per query, join algorithm, and read-only or constrained flags that prevent a user from changing a value. Profiles attach to users or roles and can be overridden for one session or query. The server resolves the effective value by a fixed order and records where it came from. Hard cases come from interaction: a setting that is harmless alone, such as a very large thread count, multiplies with concurrency into oversubscription, and settings changed per session can leak through pooled connections.",
    k: ["Profiles per user or role vs settings per query: profiles give central control and constraints; per-query settings let experts tune a single report but can bypass intent.", "Constrained settings (min, max, read-only) vs free settings: constraints stop a user from lifting their own limits, but require someone to maintain the bounds as workloads change."],
    p: ["A pooled connection keeps one client's session override and applies it to the next unrelated client.", "Raising a parallelism setting for one report is copied to all users and causes CPU oversubscription at peak."],
    v: "Assign a profile with a memory cap to a role, log in as that role, try to raise the cap in-session and confirm the change is refused, then check the system settings view shows the profile as the source.",
    dep: ["session-settings", "access-control"]
  },
  "access-control": {
    s: "Users, roles and grants that decide who may read, insert into or alter which databases, tables and columns, evaluated before any data is read.",
    d: "Identities are authenticated at connection time and mapped to roles, which hold grants on objects down to the column. At analysis time the planner checks every referenced table and column against the effective grants and fails the query before execution begins. Roles nest and can be switched in session. Grants are stored in the catalog and replicated in a cluster. The difficulty is coverage: views and materialized views need a rule for whether they run with the caller's or the definer's rights, and background paths such as merges and replication run internally with no user.",
    k: ["Column-level grants vs table-level grants: column grants protect sensitive fields precisely but multiply rules and make audits harder to read.", "Definer's rights vs caller's rights on views: definer's rights let a view expose a safe slice of a restricted table, but a badly written view then becomes a privilege escalation."],
    p: ["A grant on a table is assumed to cover a materialized view fed from it, but the view's target table has its own grants, so data leaks or is blocked unexpectedly.", "Access rules held per node drift apart in a cluster, so a user is allowed on one node and refused on another."],
    v: "Grant a role select on two columns of a table, then confirm that selecting those columns works, selecting a third fails at analysis with no scan started, and select-star is refused.",
    dep: ["parser-and-analyzer", "cluster-metadata"]
  },
  "row-policies": {
    s: "Filter expressions attached to a table and a role that are added to every query, so each user sees only the rows they are allowed to, with no change to the client query.",
    d: "A policy is a boolean predicate on table columns, such as the tenant id equals the user's tenant. During analysis the predicate is combined with the query's own filter and pushed into the scan, where it can use the sparse index and min-max summaries like any other filter. Policies from several rules combine by defined logic. Because it is enforced inside the engine, it covers every access route. The danger is leakage through side channels: error messages, approximate functions and timing may reveal that hidden rows exist.",
    k: ["Policy as an injected filter vs separate views per tenant: injected filters need one table and one set of storage, but views allow different schemas and are simpler to audit.", "Policy applied at scan vs applied after joins: at the scan is cheap and safe from reordering, but needs the predicate to reference only that table's columns."],
    p: ["A policy on the fact table is forgotten on a joined dimension table, and the join exposes a protected attribute.", "The policy column is not in the sort key, so every tenant's query scans the whole table and the cost grows with total data."],
    v: "As two tenants, run the same count query and confirm each gets only its rows, the plan shows the policy predicate in the scan filter, and the granules-read counter is far below the table total.",
    dep: ["access-control", "predicate-pushdown", "sparse-primary-index"]
  },
  "column-masking": {
    s: "Replaces a sensitive column's value with a redacted, hashed or truncated form for roles not cleared to see it, evaluated as part of the query rather than stored separately.",
    d: "A masking rule maps a column and a role to an expression, for example the last four digits only, a keyed hash, or null. The analyzer substitutes the expression wherever the column is referenced in output. For filters and joins, the policy decides whether the unmasked value may be used internally: a hashed value still supports equality joins without revealing the original. The challenge is that masked values can be reconstructed through repeated filtering, so rules must also limit what predicates are allowed on the column.",
    k: ["Deterministic masking vs random masking: deterministic hashes keep joins and group-bys useful but allow guessing against small value sets; random values are safe but break analysis.", "Mask at query time vs store a masked copy: query-time masking has one source of truth, while a masked copy is faster to read and cannot leak but doubles storage and needs refreshing."],
    p: ["A user who may filter on the masked column recovers values one by one with repeated range queries.", "The raw column leaks in an error message or a query log entry because logs record the unmasked literal."],
    v: "As a restricted role, select a masked card column and confirm only the masked form appears, then group by the same column and confirm groups are the masked values and the raw value is absent from the query log.",
    dep: ["access-control", "parser-and-analyzer"]
  },
  "encryption-at-rest": {
    s: "Encrypts column files and metadata on disk or object storage with keys held outside the data, so lost disks or a storage provider cannot read the contents.",
    d: "Data is encrypted in blocks after compression and before it is written. Keys are fetched from an external key service, held in memory, and wrapped so the data key can be rotated without rewriting everything. Encrypting after compression matters: encrypted bytes do not compress. The cost is CPU on every read of cold data, partly offset by hardware instructions. Hard questions are around rotation and recovery: lost keys mean unrecoverable data, and a key that has to be reachable at startup becomes an availability dependency.",
    k: ["Encrypt blocks vs encrypt whole volumes: block encryption protects data even on object storage and across tiers, but costs CPU in the engine; volume encryption is transparent but protects only that device.", "Per-table keys vs one key per cluster: per-table keys limit exposure and allow deletion by destroying the key, at the cost of key management and slower reads of cold keys."],
    p: ["The key service is down at restart and the whole cluster cannot open its tables.", "Compression is run after encryption by a misconfigured codec chain, so the data no longer shrinks and storage costs jump."],
    v: "Write a table with a known marker string, search the raw files on disk for the marker and confirm no match, then rotate the key, restart, and confirm queries still succeed and the files remain unreadable without the key.",
    dep: ["general-codecs", "column-files"]
  },
  "query-audit-log": {
    s: "Durable record of who ran which statement, from where, when, against which tables, and with what outcome, kept for security review and compliance.",
    d: "Each finished or failed statement writes an entry with user, role, client address, normalized query text, tables and columns accessed, row and byte counts, and error code. Entries go to an append-only table or external sink. Reading data is logged as well as changing it, which differs from a transactional system where mostly writes matter. The hard parts are volume and content: logging literals may record the very values being protected, and a log held in the same system an attacker administers can be edited.",
    k: ["Log full text with literals vs normalized text with hashed parameters: full text makes investigations exact but copies sensitive values into the log; normalized text is safer but cannot answer which value was looked up.", "Synchronous audit write vs batched write: synchronous guarantees nothing is lost but slows each statement; batching is fast but loses the final entries in a crash."],
    p: ["Audit rows contain literal customer identifiers, so the log becomes a more sensitive store than the data.", "Failed attempts are not logged because the check ran before the audit hook, hiding probing."],
    v: "Run a successful query, a denied query and a cancelled query as one user, then confirm all three appear with outcomes, the tables and columns touched, and that tampering with the table by a normal admin is refused or flagged.",
    dep: ["access-control", "system-metrics-tables"]
  },
  "client-protocols": {
    s: "The wire protocols clients use to submit queries and receive results: a native binary protocol that sends blocks of columns, plus compatibility layers for standard database drivers.",
    d: "The native protocol frames a query, settings and external tables, then streams back typed column blocks with progress and profile packets, so a client can display progress and decode directly into arrays. Compatibility protocols mimic the wire format of common row databases, letting existing drivers connect, but must convert column blocks to rows. Compression of the stream is optional. The challenge is that row-oriented drivers expect small results and per-row calls, so a large result through them can cost far more in conversion than in query execution.",
    k: ["Native column-block protocol vs a row-database-compatible protocol: the native one is fastest and carries progress, but needs a specific driver; compatibility works with existing tools at a conversion cost.", "Compressing the stream vs sending raw: compression saves network for large results across regions but costs CPU on both ends and gives little on already-small replies."],
    p: ["A reporting tool pulling a hundred million rows through a row-compatible driver spends minutes converting values in the client.", "Idle connections hold server threads and settings and are never closed by a pool that lacks a timeout."],
    v: "Fetch the same ten-million-row result through the native client and a compatibility driver and confirm both return identical values, with the native path measurably faster and the stream reporting progress packets mid-query.",
    dep: ["result-streaming", "session-settings"]
  },
  "http-interface": {
    s: "A plain request and response interface over HTTP where a statement is posted and results come back in a chosen format, used by scripts, proxies and browsers.",
    d: "A client sends the statement in the body or query string and settings as parameters, and receives the result in the response with the format named in a parameter. Credentials travel as headers. Because it is HTTP, it passes through load balancers and proxies and works from any language. Large inserts can stream the body in chunks. Problems arise from the protocol: a response begins before the query finishes, so an error mid-query appears after a 200 status has already been sent, and clients must read the trailer or an in-band error marker.",
    k: ["Stream results as they are produced vs buffer then reply: streaming starts fast and bounds memory, but a late error cannot change the status code already sent.", "Statement in the query string vs in the body: the query string is simple to log and cache but exposes text in access logs and has length limits."],
    p: ["A client trusts a 200 status and parses a truncated response as complete because the real error came as text at the end.", "Statements in URLs containing secrets or personal data end up in proxy access logs."],
    v: "Post a query that fails halfway through a large scan and confirm the client detects the in-band error marker or trailer rather than accepting the partial output, and that the server stopped scanning after the client disconnected.",
    dep: ["result-streaming", "query-cancellation", "access-control"]
  },
  "result-streaming": {
    s: "Sends result blocks to the client as soon as they are ready, in formats chosen per request such as binary columns, CSV, JSON lines or open columnar files.",
    d: "The final operator of the pipeline writes each block to an output formatter, which serializes it and pushes it to the socket. Back-pressure from a slow client stalls the pipeline through bounded buffers, so memory stays flat. Column-oriented formats can write blocks directly, while row formats transpose each block. Sorted results or final aggregations cannot stream until the last input is seen. The hard part is holding resources: a slow client keeps a query's memory, locks on parts and threads alive for as long as it takes to read.",
    k: ["Bounded buffers with back-pressure vs unbounded buffering: bounded buffers cap memory but let a slow client stall the query; unbounded buffering frees the query but can exhaust memory.", "Column-native output format vs row text format: native formats are compact and fast to produce, but row text formats are readable and accepted by every tool."],
    p: ["A slow dashboard client holds a heavy query's memory and its source parts for minutes after the work is otherwise done.", "A formatter that builds the entire result in memory first defeats streaming and fails on large outputs."],
    v: "Select a billion-row table with a stalled client and confirm server memory stays flat at the buffer size, then resume the client and confirm all rows arrive in order without duplication.",
    dep: ["column-batches", "pipelined-operators"]
  },
  "session-settings": {
    s: "Per-connection state such as the current database, time zone and setting overrides that persist across statements and alter how later queries plan and run.",
    d: "Session state is held on the server for the life of a connection or in a session identifier on stateless interfaces. It carries the default database, a time zone for time functions, optimizer toggles, limits and temporary tables. Settings override the user's profile within what its constraints allow. The difficulty is statefulness: pooled and load-balanced connections may land on different servers, so a setting applied on one is missing on the next, and a hidden override can change results, such as a different time zone or a different join algorithm.",
    k: ["Server-side session state vs settings sent with every request: session state saves bytes and allows temporary tables, but breaks with load balancers; sending per request is stateless and robust but verbose.", "Allow all overrides vs constrain them by profile: freedom helps analysts experiment, while constraints keep results and costs predictable for shared users."],
    p: ["A time zone set in one session silently changes the bucket boundaries of a daily report depending on which connection ran it.", "Temporary tables vanish when a balancer reroutes the next statement to another node."],
    v: "Set a time zone in one session, run a daily rollup, open a second session with a default time zone and confirm the results differ in the expected buckets, then confirm both settings show in the session view.",
    dep: ["configuration-profiles", "client-protocols"]
  },
  "query-cancellation": {
    s: "Stops a running query on request, on timeout or on client disconnect, releasing its threads, memory and remote work across every node it touched.",
    d: "Every query carries a cancellation flag checked by operators between blocks. A cancel request, a timeout, a client disconnect or a limit breach sets the flag, and pipelines unwind at the next check. For distributed queries the coordinator sends a cancel to each shard, which stops the sub-query and its scans. Because operators process thousands of values per check, cancellation is not instant: a long single call, such as a huge sort or a remote read, can run past the request. Merges are cancelled differently and may be allowed to finish.",
    k: ["Cooperative checks between blocks vs forceful thread termination: cooperative checks are safe and release resources cleanly but respond slowly during long steps; forced kills are fast but can leave locks and memory.", "Cancel on client disconnect vs let the query finish: cancelling frees resources when nobody waits, but a flaky network can abort an expensive report that someone wanted."],
    p: ["A timeout cancels the coordinator but the shard sub-queries keep running because the cancel message was lost.", "Cancelling a query during a large hash build leaves memory held until the operator returns from its current call."],
    v: "Start a long distributed aggregation, kill the client connection, and confirm within a few seconds that the running-queries view is empty on every node and memory use drops back to its pre-query level.",
    dep: ["pipelined-operators", "query-fanout"]
  },
  "parser-and-analyzer": {
    s: "Turns SQL text into a syntax tree, then resolves every table, column, function and type against the catalog and checks permissions, producing a validated logical query.",
    d: "The parser builds a tree from the statement. The analyzer binds names to catalog objects, expands views and aliases, infers and checks column types, resolves function overloads, applies row policies and masks, and rejects unauthorized references. For analytical SQL it must also handle wide queries with hundreds of expressions, array and nested-type functions, and window specifications. The output is a typed tree with every reference resolved, ready to be rewritten and costed. Its difficulty is semantic detail: implicit casts, null behavior and ambiguous names must match a specified dialect exactly or results differ from users' expectations.",
    k: ["Strict typing with explicit casts vs permissive implicit conversion: strictness catches mistakes and keeps predicates on sort-key columns usable, while permissiveness is friendlier to analysts but can disable pruning silently.", "Resolve names once at analysis vs lazily at execution: eager resolution catches errors before cluster work starts, but must be repeated if the schema changes between analysis and run."],
    p: ["An implicit cast on a filter column makes the predicate non-sargable, so the sparse index is bypassed and the query scans everything.", "A view definition is re-expanded against a changed base table and the query silently returns different columns."],
    v: "Submit a query with a misspelled column, an ambiguous join column and a forbidden table and confirm each is rejected at analysis with a specific message before any scan or remote call starts.",
    dep: ["access-control", "row-policies", "column-masking"]
  },
  "logical-rewrites": {
    s: "Rule-based transformations of the query tree into cheaper equivalents: constant folding, subquery unnesting, redundant join removal, common subexpression sharing and aggregation simplification.",
    d: "Rules fire on tree patterns until no more apply. Typical ones fold constants, convert correlated subqueries to joins, drop columns never used, remove joins whose result cannot change the output, merge adjacent filters or projections, and simplify aggregate functions such as turning a count of a non-null column into a plain count. Each rule must preserve results exactly, including null semantics and duplicates. The difficulty is ordering and interaction: rules enable one another, some conflict, and an unsafe rule may be wrong only on edge cases such as empty groups or nulls.",
    k: ["Heuristic rules always applied vs cost-based choice per rewrite: rules are fast and predictable but can pick a worse plan on unusual data; costing each alternative is better informed but multiplies planning time.", "Run rewrites to fixpoint vs a fixed number of passes: fixpoint finds all simplifications but can loop on interacting rules, while fixed passes terminate but may stop early."],
    p: ["A subquery unnesting rule changes results when the subquery returns duplicates or nulls, producing a count that is subtly wrong.", "A rewrite that moves an expression below a join evaluates it on far more rows than the original."],
    v: "Take a corpus of queries, run each with rewrites on and off against the same data, and confirm identical results, and that rewritten plans for a known set show fewer operators or fewer rows scanned.",
    dep: ["parser-and-analyzer", "predicate-pushdown"]
  },
  "predicate-pushdown": {
    s: "Moves filter conditions as close to the data as possible, ideally into the scan itself, so rows and whole blocks are rejected before any other work.",
    d: "Conjunctions in the where clause are split and pushed below joins, projections and unions where it is safe, then converted into a scan filter. At the scan, each condition becomes a test against the sparse index, min-max summaries and skip indexes to exclude blocks, and later a row-level filter on the surviving blocks. Pushing through outer joins and window functions needs care, since a filter on the nullable side changes semantics. The hard part is knowing which conditions are cheap and selective enough to run first.",
    k: ["Push all conjuncts down vs keep expensive ones high: pushing every filter reduces data early, but an expensive function evaluated at the scan on every row can cost more than it saves.", "Derive implied predicates vs use only stated ones: deriving a filter on a join key from the other side's filter prunes more data, but relies on correct equality and type reasoning."],
    p: ["A filter on the nullable side of a left join is pushed below it, turning the outer join into an inner one and dropping rows.", "A predicate using a non-deterministic or stateful function is pushed down and evaluated a different number of times than written."],
    v: "Run a join query with a filter on the fact table and confirm in the plan the filter appears in the scan, the granules-read counter is far below the table total, and results equal a run with pushdown disabled.",
    dep: ["logical-rewrites", "column-statistics"]
  },
  "column-statistics": {
    s: "Per-column summaries such as row counts, distinct counts, null fractions, min and max values and histograms, used by the planner to estimate selectivity and cost.",
    d: "Some statistics are free by-products of storage: every part records its row count and each block's min and max. Others, such as distinct counts and histograms, are collected by sampling or sketch building during ingestion or in a separate analyze step and merged across parts and shards. Estimators read them at plan time. The challenge is freshness and cost: statistics for a table that gets new parts every second go stale between collections, and exact distinct counts over billions of values are expensive, so sketches with bounded error are used.",
    k: ["Collect during ingestion vs a separate analyze job: ingestion-time collection is always current and incremental, but slows inserts; separate jobs are cheap on writes yet run behind the data.", "Exact statistics vs sketches: exact values give better plans but cost memory and time on large columns; sketches are small and mergeable but add error that compounds in estimates."],
    p: ["A table loaded in the last hour has no statistics, so the planner assumes defaults and picks a nested broadcast of the largest table.", "Statistics from one shard are used to represent the whole table and are far off when data is skewed."],
    v: "Load a table with a known skewed column, collect statistics and confirm the stored distinct count and histogram are within the sketch's error bound of exact values computed by brute force.",
    dep: ["approx-distinct", "file-footer-metadata", "data-parts"]
  },
  "cardinality-estimation": {
    s: "Predicts how many rows each filter, join and aggregation will produce, which drives every cost decision; errors multiply through a plan.",
    d: "Estimators combine column statistics with assumptions: independence between predicates, uniform spread within a histogram bucket, and containment of join keys. The estimated row count for each operator feeds join order, join side choice and memory reservations. Correlated columns, such as city and country, break independence and underestimate drastically, and joins amplify errors at every step. Some engines compensate with runtime feedback, adaptive choices at execution, or multi-column statistics. The difficulty is that bad estimates produce plans that are catastrophically slow, not merely a bit worse.",
    k: ["Independence assumption vs multi-column statistics: independence needs no extra data and is cheap, but fails on correlated columns; multi-column statistics fix known pairs at storage and maintenance cost.", "Trust estimates vs adapt at runtime: trusting is simple and fast to plan, while runtime adaptation corrects a wrong build-side choice but needs operators that can change plans mid-query."],
    p: ["A two-predicate filter on correlated columns is estimated at one row when millions match, and a broadcast of the large side overwhelms memory.", "After a data shift, estimates remain based on old histograms and plan regressions appear with no code change."],
    v: "For a set of queries, compare the plan's estimated rows to actual rows per operator in the profile, and confirm the ratio is within a small factor for common predicates and flagged for correlated predicate pairs.",
    dep: ["column-statistics", "query-profiler"]
  },
  "join-ordering": {
    s: "Chooses the order and algorithm of joins in a multi-table query and which side to build, using estimated cardinalities, so intermediate results stay small.",
    d: "Star and snowflake queries join a large fact table to small dimension tables. The planner enumerates orders, using dynamic programming for a few tables and heuristics beyond that, and costs each by estimated intermediate sizes and the algorithm available. It puts the smaller input on the build side of a hash join and places the largest table on the probe side so it streams. Join order also decides which runtime filters can be built early. The difficulty is search space size and estimate error: with many joins no search is exhaustive, and the cheapest plan on paper may not be the best one.",
    k: ["Exhaustive search for small joins vs greedy heuristics: exhaustive search finds the best estimated plan but grows exponentially; heuristics handle dozens of tables quickly and can miss a much better order.", "Follow the written order vs reorder freely: following it respects user intent and is predictable; reordering usually wins but can make a query that worked suddenly different."],
    p: ["The planner picks the larger table as the hash build side because of a stale row estimate, and runs out of memory.", "A query with sixty joins spends longer planning than executing."],
    v: "Run a star query with joins written in the worst order, and confirm the chosen plan builds on the small dimension tables, probes with the fact table, and runs within a small factor of a hand-tuned order.",
    dep: ["cardinality-estimation", "hash-join", "runtime-filters"]
  },
  "distributed-planner": {
    s: "Splits a plan into fragments that run on shards and a final step on the coordinator, deciding what is computed locally, what is moved and what is merged.",
    d: "Given the table distribution, the planner pushes as much as possible to the shards: scans, filters, partial aggregations and, when keys are co-located, joins. Fragments exchange data by broadcast, hash shuffle or gather to the coordinator, and the plan includes the merge step that completes each partial result. It also picks replicas and the degree of parallelism. The challenge is communication: moving data between nodes costs more than computing, so plan shape depends on how tables were distributed and on estimated sizes, and a poor choice moves terabytes.",
    k: ["Push work to shards and merge on the coordinator vs shuffle by key: coordinator merging is simple and good for small results, but a high-cardinality aggregation overwhelms it, so shuffling spreads the work.", "Co-located joins vs flexible distribution: co-location avoids data movement but ties tables to one distribution key; flexibility serves more queries and moves more data."],
    p: ["A global distinct count is gathered raw to the coordinator and exhausts its memory instead of merging per-shard partial sets.", "Two tables intended to be co-located use different distribution functions and the join silently becomes a full shuffle."],
    v: "Run a group-by over a distributed table and confirm the plan shows per-shard partial aggregation, a merge on the coordinator, and bytes transferred close to partial result size rather than raw rows.",
    dep: ["distribution-key", "query-fanout", "distributed-join-strategies", "partial-aggregation"]
  },
  "column-batches": {
    s: "The unit of data passed between operators: a block of a few thousand values per column in contiguous arrays, not individual rows.",
    d: "A batch holds a fixed number of rows as one array per column, with a validity bitmap for nulls and sometimes a dictionary or constant form for compact cases. Operators loop over arrays with simple code, which amortizes call overhead and keeps data in CPU cache. The batch size trades cache fit against overhead: too small wastes time on call overhead, too large spills out of the cache. Variable-length values use offsets into a shared buffer. The difficulty is keeping the interfaces clean while allowing special forms, such as a column that is constant for the whole batch, so operators avoid expanding it.",
    k: ["Batches of thousands of rows vs a whole column at once: batches fit in cache and stream with bounded memory; whole columns are simpler but materialize large intermediates and defeat pipelining.", "Allow compact forms (constant, dictionary) vs always flat arrays: compact forms save work and memory but every operator must handle each form or convert."],
    p: ["An operator expands a dictionary-encoded column to flat strings, silently multiplying memory and time.", "Batch size set for one workload is too small for wide rows and produces many tiny batches with high overhead."],
    v: "Profile a scan-filter-aggregate query and confirm operators handle batches of the configured size, the batch count matches rows divided by batch size, and cache-miss counters stay low relative to a row-at-a-time baseline.",
    dep: ["validity-bitmaps", "variable-length-data"]
  },
  "pipelined-operators": {
    s: "Operators arranged into pipelines that pass batches directly from scan to filter to partial aggregate without storing intermediate results, breaking only where an operator needs all its input.",
    d: "A plan is cut into pipelines at operators that must consume everything before output, such as a hash build, a sort or a final aggregation. Within a pipeline, a batch flows from the source through each operator in turn, in cache. Pipelines run on several threads, each pulling its own batches, with a pipeline's output feeding the next one's input. Scheduling must respect dependencies between pipelines. The difficulty is blocking: a pipeline breaker must hold all state, which is where memory pressure arises, and an imbalance between pipelines leaves cores idle.",
    k: ["Pull-based vs push-based flow: pull is simple to write and cancel, while push avoids repeated calls and lets one batch feed several consumers, at the cost of harder flow control.", "Fuse operators into one loop vs keep them separate: fusing avoids writing intermediate batches but is complex to build; separate operators are modular and easy to profile."],
    p: ["A pipeline breaker holds every row of a large input and the query fails far from the operator that is actually responsible.", "One operator that is single-threaded caps the speed of an otherwise parallel pipeline."],
    v: "Run a scan, filter and aggregation and confirm the profile shows one pipeline with no intermediate materialization, all worker threads busy for most of the run, and peak memory proportional to the group count, not the input size.",
    dep: ["column-batches", "morsel-scheduling"]
  },
  "simd-kernels": {
    s: "Tight, branch-free loops that apply one operation to many values at once with vector instructions, used for filters, arithmetic, comparisons and hashing.",
    d: "Each kernel handles one operation on one type over an array: compare a column to a constant and produce a bitmask, add two columns, or hash a key column. Compilers or hand-written code use vector instructions, and the engine picks a variant at startup based on what the CPU supports. Kernels avoid branches by computing masks and selecting results. Mixed types, null handling and overflow checks complicate them, because each adds a variant. The challenge is the number of combinations of types, operations and encodings, and keeping every variant correct.",
    k: ["Hand-tuned kernels per type vs generic code left to the compiler: hand-tuned kernels are fastest on key paths, but multiply code to maintain; generic code is smaller and slower on hot loops.", "Check overflow on every operation vs assume no overflow: checked arithmetic prevents silent wrong answers but is slower and blocks some vectorization."],
    p: ["A rarely used kernel variant for one integer width has an off-by-one in the tail handling and returns wrong rows for batches not divisible by the vector width.", "Dispatch picks a vector instruction set that the host reports but a virtualized environment does not actually support."],
    v: "Run each kernel on random arrays whose lengths include every remainder modulo the vector width and nulls at the edges, and confirm output equals a plain scalar reference on all inputs.",
    dep: ["column-batches", "validity-bitmaps"]
  },
  "selection-vectors": {
    s: "A list or bitmask marking which rows of a batch passed earlier filters, so later operators handle only those rows without copying the batch.",
    d: "After a filter, instead of building new smaller columns, the engine attaches a selection to the batch, either a list of surviving row indexes or a bitmap. Subsequent operators read through it. When the selection becomes sparse, the batch is compacted to restore dense arrays. Indexes are efficient for very selective filters, bitmaps for moderate ones. The difficulty is deciding when to compact: reading through a sparse selection wastes memory bandwidth, but compacting copies every column.",
    k: ["Index list vs bitmap: lists are compact and fast for selective filters, while bitmaps are better for moderately selective ones and vectorize well, so engines often support both.", "Compact eagerly vs carry the selection: compacting makes later work simple and dense but copies data; carrying avoids copies but later operators touch more memory than needed."],
    p: ["A chain of filters leaves a very sparse selection, and each following operator still reads full cache lines.", "An operator ignores the selection and processes filtered-out rows, giving wrong aggregates."],
    v: "Apply a filter keeping one percent of rows, then a sum, and confirm the sum equals a reference over only kept rows, while counters show downstream operators processed only the selected rows.",
    dep: ["column-batches", "simd-kernels"]
  },
  "late-materialization": {
    s: "Delays reading or assembling the columns that are not needed yet, working first on row positions from filter columns and fetching other columns only for rows that survive.",
    d: "A query filters on a few cheap columns first, producing positions of matching rows. Only then are the remaining columns read, and only for the blocks and positions that matched. Joins can run on key columns alone, producing pairs of positions, and the payload columns are fetched afterward. The saving is large when filters are selective and columns are wide, such as long strings. Late work is not always better: if most rows survive, positional fetch adds overhead, and for a join, fetching payloads in an order unrelated to their storage causes random reads.",
    k: ["Fetch columns late by position vs read everything up front: late fetching skips most I/O for selective queries, but random positional reads can cost more than a sequential scan when selectivity is low.", "Materialize after the join vs before it: after the join, only matching rows are assembled, but the position lists add memory and an extra step for each payload column."],
    p: ["A join returns positions in probe order, and fetching a wide payload column in that order makes random reads that run slower than reading the whole column.", "The filter is estimated selective but is not, and the late fetch path becomes the slowest part of the query."],
    v: "Run a selective filter with a wide string column in the select list, and confirm the profile shows the string column read only for blocks containing matches, with bytes read a small fraction of the column's size.",
    dep: ["prewhere-filtering", "row-position-mapping", "selection-vectors"]
  },
  "morsel-scheduling": {
    s: "Divides input into small chunks and lets each worker thread take the next chunk when it is free, so all cores stay busy even when chunks differ in cost.",
    d: "The scan splits its ranges into morsels of a fixed row count or byte size. Worker threads pull morsels from a shared queue, run the full pipeline on each, and write into thread-local state that is merged at the end. Preferring morsels from memory local to a core's socket reduces memory traffic. The scheduler adjusts the number of threads per query to the load. Skew is the hard part: one expensive morsel, such as one containing heavy aggregation keys, can leave a single thread running while others wait, and a small chunk size trades balance against scheduling overhead.",
    k: ["Dynamic morsel pulling vs static partitioning of input: pulling balances uneven work automatically but needs a shared queue; static partitions are simpler but one slow partition delays the whole query.", "Many threads per query vs fewer: more threads finish one query sooner but fight other queries for cores and cache."],
    p: ["Thread-local aggregation state gets large per thread and the merge at the end becomes the serial bottleneck.", "Each concurrent query spawns as many threads as cores, and context switching degrades throughput under load."],
    v: "Scan a table whose first half is cheap rows and second half expensive, and confirm every worker thread is busy until near the end, with the profile showing similar processed morsel counts weighted by cost.",
    dep: ["pipelined-operators", "parallel-range-scans"]
  },
  "runtime-compilation": {
    s: "Generates machine code for a query's expressions or whole pipeline at run time, removing interpretation overhead for long-running queries on complex expressions.",
    d: "For expression trees with many operations, the engine can compile them into a single fused function that evaluates them over a batch with values kept in registers. Compilation takes tens to hundreds of milliseconds, so it pays only when the query runs long enough or the same expression recurs, and compiled code can be cached by expression fingerprint. Vectorized interpretation already removes most overhead, so the gain is mainly on compound expressions. The difficulty is the added complexity: a second execution path to keep identical in results, compile-time latency, and harder debugging.",
    k: ["Compile fused code vs rely on pre-built vector kernels: compiling gains on complex expressions but adds compile time and a second code path; pre-built kernels start instantly and are easier to verify.", "Compile in the background and switch when ready vs wait: switching hides latency but needs both paths to run correctly mid-query."],
    p: ["Compilation takes longer than the query, so short queries get slower after the feature is turned on.", "Compiled and interpreted paths differ on an edge case such as overflow and give different answers between runs."],
    v: "Run an expression-heavy query ten times with compilation on and off, confirm identical results, and that compilation time is recorded once and later runs reuse cached code with lower latency.",
    dep: ["simd-kernels", "column-batches"]
  },
  "spill-to-disk": {
    s: "Writes operator state such as hash tables, sorted runs and intermediate partitions to temporary files when memory runs out, so large queries finish slowly instead of failing.",
    d: "When an operator exceeds its memory grant, it partitions its state by hash or writes sorted runs to temporary storage, then processes them partition by partition or merges runs. Aggregation spills partial groups, hash join spills build and probe partitions, sort writes runs and merges them. Spilled data is written in the engine's compressed block format. The hard part is predictability: spilling may recurse if a partition is still too big, the amount of I/O can be many times the input, and fast temporary storage may not exist on the node.",
    k: ["Spill and continue vs fail with an out-of-memory error: spilling lets large queries finish and protects other queries, but can make a query orders of magnitude slower; failing is fast and visible.", "Partition early by hash vs spill on demand: early partitioning makes spill cheap but adds overhead to queries that would have fit in memory; spilling on demand costs nothing until memory is full."],
    p: ["Temporary files fill the same disk as table data and stop ingestion.", "A heavily skewed key makes one partition larger than memory, forcing recursive spilling with large I/O amplification."],
    v: "Run a high-cardinality group-by with a small memory limit, confirm it completes with the profile showing spilled bytes, temporary files are removed after the query, and results equal those of a run with ample memory.",
    dep: ["memory-accounting", "hash-aggregation", "hash-join", "top-n"]
  },
  "hash-aggregation": {
    s: "Groups rows by key using a hash table of running aggregate states, updated as batches stream through, the standard way to run group-by on columnar data.",
    d: "For each batch, the key columns are hashed together, the table is probed for each row's group, and per-group states are updated by vectorized functions: sum, count, min, max and sketches. Specialized tables handle small integer keys, single string keys and many-column keys differently, and low-cardinality cases can use a direct array. The table grows as new groups appear and resizing is expensive. The difficulty is the number of groups: a few groups fit in cache and run extremely fast, while hundreds of millions need partitioned or spilled tables and dominate query cost.",
    k: ["Hash aggregation vs sort-based aggregation: hashing is faster with unordered input and many groups, but needs memory for every group; sorting uses less memory with ordered input and is slower otherwise.", "One shared table vs per-thread tables merged at the end: per-thread tables avoid contention and scale, but use memory on every thread and require a merge, while a shared table saves memory but contends."],
    p: ["A high-cardinality key makes the table many times larger than expected and the query is killed by its memory limit.", "Floating point sums vary slightly between runs because threads merge states in a different order."],
    v: "Group a billion-row table by a low-cardinality key and by a unique key, and confirm both match a reference result, with throughput high for the first and spilling or partitioning visible in the profile for the second.",
    dep: ["column-batches", "morsel-scheduling", "spill-to-disk"]
  },
  "partial-aggregation": {
    s: "Aggregates locally first into small partial states, sends those partial states instead of raw rows, and combines them in a final step, reducing data moved between threads and nodes.",
    d: "Each thread, then each shard, produces partial states per group, such as a running sum and count for an average. These states are merged by the same function that updates them: merge of two sums is their sum, of two sketches is their union. The final step combines states across threads and nodes and then finalizes values. This works only for functions with mergeable states, so some require special algorithms. The hard part is when partial aggregation does not help: with nearly unique keys, partial results are as large as the input, and the extra step wastes effort.",
    k: ["Aggregate before moving data vs ship raw rows: partial aggregation cuts network traffic by orders of magnitude when groups are few, but wastes work when keys are nearly unique.", "Merge states on the coordinator vs shuffle states by key: coordinator merging is simple but a bottleneck for many groups, while shuffling by key distributes the final step at the cost of another exchange."],
    p: ["Aggregate functions without a merge rule, such as an exact median, force all raw values to one node.", "Partial states for distinct counts are exact sets, which are far larger than the raw column when values are mostly unique."],
    v: "Run an average and a distinct count over a sharded table and confirm bytes exchanged are proportional to groups times state size, and results match a single-node run on the same data.",
    dep: ["hash-aggregation", "query-fanout", "approx-distinct"]
  },
  "high-cardinality-grouping": {
    s: "Techniques for group-by on keys with many distinct values: partitioning the hash table, two-level aggregation, radix partitioning and spilling, so the groups need not fit in cache or memory.",
    d: "When groups exceed cache, a flat hash table suffers cache misses on every probe. Two-level or radix schemes first split rows by a few bits of the hash into partitions that each fit in cache or memory, then aggregate each partition independently, which also parallelizes the merge across threads. Partitions may spill. Some engines convert from a single table to a partitioned one when the group count crosses a threshold. The challenge is the switch: deciding too early costs for small queries, too late wastes work already done.",
    k: ["Single hash table that converts to two-level when large vs always partitioned: converting keeps small queries fast but adds a transition; always partitioned is simpler and slower for few groups.", "Parallel merge by partition vs serial merge: merging partitions in parallel scales with cores, but needs all partial tables partitioned the same way."],
    p: ["Data with a heavy key puts most rows in one partition, so the merge step has one busy thread and the rest wait.", "The conversion threshold is tuned for one machine and thrashes caches on hardware with a smaller cache."],
    v: "Group ten billion rows by an almost unique key, and confirm the profile shows partitioned aggregation, even partition sizes, merge time spread across threads and a final result equal to a reference count.",
    dep: ["hash-aggregation", "spill-to-disk", "morsel-scheduling"]
  },
  "hash-join": {
    s: "Builds a hash table from the smaller input's join keys and streams the larger input through it, the main join algorithm for star-schema queries.",
    d: "The build phase reads the smaller table, hashes the key columns and stores keys and needed payload columns. The probe phase hashes each batch of the larger input, looks up matches and emits joined columns. Hash tables are built in parallel by partitioning or lock-free insertion. Variations handle semi, anti and outer joins and joins on multiple conditions. The difficulty is memory: the build side must fit or be partitioned and spilled, and a many-to-many join can produce output far larger than either input, so memory for output also needs planning.",
    k: ["Build on the smaller side vs the larger: building on the smaller side saves memory and builds fast, but depends on size estimates that may be wrong.", "Store payload in the table vs store row positions and fetch later: payload in the table is simple and fast to read, while positions keep the table small and defer reading wide columns."],
    p: ["A key that matches millions of rows on both sides explodes the output, turning a join into a runaway query.", "A wrong estimate picks the large table as the build side and the hash table does not fit."],
    v: "Join a billion-row fact table to a million-row dimension and confirm the profile shows build on the dimension, probe on the fact, build memory proportional to the dimension, and results matching a nested reference on a sample.",
    dep: ["column-batches", "runtime-filters", "spill-to-disk"]
  },
  "runtime-filters": {
    s: "A compact filter built from the join's build side, such as a key range or a Bloom filter, and applied at the probe-side scan to skip rows and blocks that cannot match.",
    d: "After the build side finishes, the engine summarizes its keys into a min-max range, a bitmap of small integers, or a Bloom filter, and passes it to the scan feeding the probe side. The scan then uses it to skip granules by their min-max summaries and to filter rows before they reach the join. This turns a join with a selective dimension filter into a fast scan of just the matching part of the fact table. The difficulty is timing and coordination: the probe scan must either wait for the filter or start without it, and in a cluster the filter has to be sent to every shard.",
    k: ["Wait for the filter before scanning vs scan immediately: waiting maximizes pruning but delays the start; scanning at once starts fast and may read data the filter would have skipped.", "Exact key set vs Bloom filter vs range: an exact set filters perfectly but is large; a Bloom filter is small with false positives; a range is smallest and effective only if the keys are clustered."],
    p: ["A Bloom filter sized for too few keys saturates and passes nearly everything, adding cost for no benefit.", "In a distributed join, the filter arrives after the shards have already scanned most of their data."],
    v: "Join a large fact table with a dimension filtered to one percent of keys, and compare the scan's rows read and granules read with the runtime filter on and off, confirming a large drop with identical results.",
    dep: ["hash-join", "min-max-zone-maps", "bloom-filter-skipping"]
  },
  "distributed-join-strategies": {
    s: "The ways a join runs across nodes: broadcast the small table to every node, shuffle both sides by key, or run locally when tables are co-located.",
    d: "A broadcast join copies the small side to all nodes that hold the large side, so the large table is never moved. A shuffle join hashes both inputs by the join key and sends each row to the node responsible for that hash, so matching rows meet. A co-located join needs no movement because both tables are distributed by the join key. The planner chooses by estimated sizes. The challenge is cost misjudgement: broadcasting something larger than expected multiplies its size by the node count, and shuffles saturate the network and add a full barrier.",
    k: ["Broadcast the small table vs shuffle both: broadcast moves little when one side is small, but grows by node count and fails with a large side; shuffle scales with data and costs bandwidth.", "Plan in advance for co-location vs decide per query: planning by distribution key gives the fastest joins for known access patterns, but makes other join keys expensive."],
    p: ["A dimension table that grew tenfold is still broadcast and each node receives a copy that no longer fits in memory.", "A skewed join key sends most rows of a shuffle to one node, which then becomes the bottleneck."],
    v: "Run the same join with the small side broadcast and with forced shuffle, and confirm network bytes for broadcast equal small-table size times node count, shuffle bytes equal both sides' sizes, and both produce identical results.",
    dep: ["distributed-planner", "distribution-key", "hash-join"]
  },
  "sort-merge-join": {
    s: "Joins two inputs that are already sorted on the join key by stepping through both in order, useful when sort order is free and memory is limited.",
    d: "Both inputs are read in key order and compared in a single pass. When tables are stored sorted by the join key, no sorting or hash table is needed, so memory use is small and the join can stream. For unsorted inputs the engine sorts them first, which usually costs more than hashing. It also handles inequality and range joins better than hash. The difficulty is that it applies only when sortedness is real across all parts: a table is sorted within each part but parts overlap, so a merge across parts is needed first.",
    k: ["Merge join on stored order vs hash join: merge needs little memory and streams when both sides share a sort key, while hash is faster for unordered data and has no such prerequisite.", "Sort then merge for range joins vs nested comparison: sorting handles ranges in near-linear time, but costs a full sort of both sides."],
    p: ["Parts overlap in key ranges, so the merge reads them all at once and the assumption of sorted input is false.", "Join on a column that is sorted only within each shard turns into a global sort."],
    v: "Join two tables sorted by the same key and confirm the plan uses merge join with no sort operators, peak memory stays near a few batches, and the result equals the hash join result.",
    dep: ["sort-order-clustering", "background-merges", "top-n"]
  },
  "window-functions": {
    s: "Computes values over ordered partitions of rows, such as running totals, ranks and moving averages, without collapsing rows as grouping does.",
    d: "Rows are partitioned by the window's partition keys and sorted by its order keys, then a frame is evaluated for each row. Engines process one partition at a time or stream sorted data, maintain running state for cumulative frames, and use special structures for sliding frames to avoid recomputing from scratch. A partition has to be held in memory in full for general frames. The difficulty is skew and size: one giant partition cannot be split across threads, and without partitioning the whole table is sorted on one node.",
    k: ["Sort per window specification vs reuse one sort for compatible windows: reusing saves large sorts, but requires ordering windows by compatible keys.", "Incremental sliding state vs recompute per row: incremental is linear in rows but only works for functions that can add and remove values; recompute is general and slow."],
    p: ["A window with no partition clause sorts the whole table on a single thread and runs far longer than the rest of the query.", "A partition with hundreds of millions of rows exhausts memory because the frame needs all of it."],
    v: "Compute a running sum partitioned by customer over a large table, spot-check several customers' values against manual sums, and confirm the profile shows the sort and window operators running in parallel across partitions.",
    dep: ["top-n", "hash-aggregation", "spill-to-disk"]
  },
  "top-n": {
    s: "Finds the first N rows by an ordering and sorts large results, keeping only a small heap when a limit is present and spilling sorted runs when it is not.",
    d: "With a limit, each thread keeps a bounded heap of the best N rows seen, and the heaps are merged at the end, so the cost is a scan and a little memory. Without a limit, sort writes sorted runs in memory, spills them when full, and merges them in a final pass. Distributed queries take the top N from each shard and then merge. When the scan is already ordered by the sort key, it can stop early after N rows. The difficulty is a large offset: skipping a million rows still requires sorting them.",
    k: ["Bounded heap for limit queries vs full sort: a heap uses constant memory and finishes with the scan, while a full sort handles any size and costs far more.", "Early termination on stored order vs scanning everything: stopping after N rows can cut the query to milliseconds, but only when the sort order matches the table's sort key."],
    p: ["A deep offset used for pagination sorts and discards the entire prefix on every page request.", "Taking top N per shard with ties at the boundary returns an inconsistent result between runs."],
    v: "Select the top 100 rows of a ten-billion-row table by the sort key and confirm the query stops after reading a few granules, then by a non-key column and confirm it scans fully with memory near the heap size.",
    dep: ["sort-order-clustering", "spill-to-disk", "sparse-primary-index"]
  },
  "approx-distinct": {
    s: "Estimates the number of distinct values with a small fixed-size sketch, such as a hyperloglog, giving a few percent error instead of unbounded memory for exact counts.",
    d: "Each value is hashed and the sketch records the maximum count of leading zero bits seen in small buckets; combining the buckets gives an estimate with error that depends on bucket count. State is a few kilobytes regardless of data size, and two sketches merge by taking the maximum for each bucket, which makes it exact to distribute and to maintain incrementally. Sketches can be stored in columns and merged in rollups. The difficulty is explaining error to users and the cost of the alternative: exact distinct requires the full set of values, which for billions of unique ids is gigabytes.",
    k: ["Sketch vs exact set: a sketch uses constant memory and merges across shards, but adds about one to two percent error; an exact set is correct and grows with cardinality.", "Higher precision vs smaller state: more buckets reduce error but multiply memory per group, which matters when there are millions of groups."],
    p: ["A sketch with one-percent error is used for a billing figure where exact counts were needed.", "Small cardinalities are slightly wrong because estimators for low counts are poorly calibrated if not corrected."],
    v: "Compute distinct counts on a column of known cardinality with the sketch and an exact method, and confirm the error is within the documented bound, memory per group is constant, and merging per-shard sketches matches a global sketch.",
    dep: ["hash-aggregation", "partial-aggregation"]
  },
  "quantile-sketches": {
    s: "Estimates percentiles such as the median or the 99th with a compact summary of the value distribution, mergeable across shards, in place of sorting all values.",
    d: "A sketch keeps a small set of centroids or a bounded sample of values with weights, tuned to be most precise near the tails. Each input updates the sketch, and merges combine sketches by combining and compressing their points. The state is kilobytes, so quantiles can be computed per group over huge data in one pass. Accuracy is a relative or rank error that depends on settings. The challenge is that rank error and value error differ: a small rank error on a heavy-tailed column can still be a large difference in value.",
    k: ["Sketch vs exact quantile: sketches work in one pass with constant memory and merge across nodes, while exact quantiles require sorting or holding all values.", "Accurate in the tails vs uniform accuracy: tail-focused sketches serve latency percentiles well, but are less precise near the median."],
    p: ["Percentiles of percentiles computed by averaging per-shard results rather than merging sketches give wrong answers.", "A sketch built with too few centroids reports the same value for a wide range of the 99.9th percentile."],
    v: "Compute the median and 99th percentile on a column with a known distribution using the sketch, compare to exact sorted values, and confirm both fall within the stated error with merged shard sketches matching a single-node sketch.",
    dep: ["partial-aggregation", "hash-aggregation"]
  },
  "top-k-sketches": {
    s: "Finds the most frequent values in a column using a bounded-size counter structure, without counting every distinct value.",
    d: "A summary keeps a fixed number of candidate values and counts, evicting low counts when a new value arrives; frequent values stay and their counts are close to the truth. The result is the approximate top K with error bounds, from state that does not depend on the number of distinct values. Summaries merge across shards by adding counts and trimming. The difficulty is that results near the boundary of K can be wrong, and a value that is frequent only on one shard may be dropped from the merge.",
    k: ["Approximate top K vs exact group-by and sort: the sketch is a single pass with fixed memory, but ranks near the cutoff can be wrong; the exact version is correct and needs a counter per distinct value.", "More counters than K vs exactly K: extra counters improve accuracy of the true top K but use more memory per group."],
    p: ["Ties and near-ties at position K are reported in different order between runs because of eviction.", "Frequent values spread evenly across many shards are each below the local threshold and vanish in the merge."],
    v: "Run the sketch on a column with a known Zipf-like distribution and confirm the returned top ten match the exact top ten with counts inside the error bound, using memory independent of distinct value count.",
    dep: ["partial-aggregation", "hash-aggregation"]
  },
  "sampled-queries": {
    s: "Runs a query on a random fraction of the data, reading only that share of blocks or rows, and scales the result for fast approximate answers on huge tables.",
    d: "Sampling can be by row or by block. Row sampling reads everything and discards rows, saving compute but not I/O, while block sampling skips whole granules, saving I/O but introducing correlation. A sampling key stored in the table lets the same sample be chosen for each query consistently. Counts and sums are scaled by the inverse of the fraction, and standard errors can be estimated. The hard part is validity: sampling is wrong for rare groups, extremes and joins between independently sampled tables.",
    k: ["Sample by block vs by row: blocks save I/O and are fast but correlated, so clustered data gives misleading results; rows are unbiased but read everything.", "Consistent sample key vs random per query: a stable key makes results repeatable and joins between sampled tables valid, but needs a column designed for it."],
    p: ["A sampled count of a rare event reports zero because none of its rows was drawn.", "Two tables sampled independently and then joined retain only a fraction squared of the true matches."],
    v: "Estimate a total from a one-percent sample, repeat with different seeds, and confirm results cluster within the expected error around the exact total while the profile shows roughly one percent of granules read.",
    dep: ["scan-planning", "granules"]
  },
  "scan-planning": {
    s: "Decides which parts, files and granule ranges a query must read, using metadata and indexes, before any column data is touched, and divides the ranges among threads and nodes.",
    d: "The planner lists the table's parts from the catalog, drops those excluded by partition and part-level min-max values, then consults each remaining part's sparse index and skip indexes to produce a list of granule ranges. Adjacent ranges are merged to make reads larger. The ranges are split into morsels for threads or sent to replicas. The difficulty is metadata size: a table with millions of parts or very many tiny files makes planning itself slow, and reading the index into memory costs time on a cold start.",
    k: ["Plan from in-memory index metadata vs read indexes on demand: in-memory planning is fast and precise but costs memory proportional to table size; on-demand reads save memory at the cost of latency.", "Merge adjacent ranges aggressively vs keep them exact: merging gives larger sequential reads and fewer requests, but reads some granules that could have been skipped."],
    p: ["Thousands of tiny parts make planning take longer than scanning.", "Statistics on part metadata are stale after a merge and the plan reads parts that no longer exist."],
    v: "Query a table with a selective sort-key filter and confirm the plan reports the number of parts and granules selected out of the total, matching the filter's expected range, with planning time a small fraction of the query.",
    dep: ["sparse-primary-index", "min-max-zone-maps", "data-parts", "partition-pruning"]
  },
  "column-projection": {
    s: "Reads only the column files a query references, so a query touching three columns of a hundred-column table reads about three percent of the bytes.",
    d: "The analyzer determines exactly which columns each operator needs and removes the rest from the scan, including through views and joins. Each needed column is opened as a separate file or chunk, so reading more columns costs more files and requests, but never touches the others. Nested columns can be read by sub-field. The saving is the central advantage of the format and is largest for wide tables. The difficulty is select-star and wide analytics: reading all columns loses the benefit and adds overhead compared with a row layout.",
    k: ["One file per column vs several columns grouped per file: per-column files give the best skipping and make adding columns cheap, but multiply file count; grouped files reduce count and read unneeded columns.", "Sub-column reads of nested fields vs reading the whole structure: sub-field reads cut I/O for wide records, but need nested layouts that store each field separately."],
    p: ["A tool issues select-star on a wide table and reads every byte for a query that needed two columns.", "A view computed over many columns forces all of them to be read even when the outer query uses one."],
    v: "Run a query on three of a hundred columns and confirm in the profile that only three column files were opened, with bytes read near the sum of those columns' compressed sizes.",
    dep: ["parser-and-analyzer", "column-files"]
  },
  "prewhere-filtering": {
    s: "Reads the most selective or cheapest filter columns first, applies the filter, and only then reads the remaining columns for the blocks and rows that matched.",
    d: "The planner moves one or more where conditions into an early stage. The scan reads just those columns of each granule, evaluates the condition, and produces a mask. If a granule has no matching rows, the other columns are never read for it. If some match, the remaining columns are read and filtered by the mask. Which conditions go first is chosen by estimated selectivity and column size, preferring small columns and selective filters. The difficulty is a wrong choice: moving a barely selective filter early reads columns twice for no skipped data.",
    k: ["Automatic choice of early filter columns vs manual hint: automation adapts as data changes and needs no tuning, but can mispredict; hints give control and become stale.", "Early filter on the cheapest column vs on the most selective: cheapest cuts the first read cost, while most selective skips the most downstream work, and the best choice depends on the column sizes."],
    p: ["A filter on a low-selectivity column is chosen as the early filter and the scan reads it twice with no skipping.", "The filter column is wide text, so the first stage costs more than reading the data would have."],
    v: "Run a query filtering on a narrow selective column and selecting wide columns, then confirm bytes read for the wide columns drop to the matching granules, and compare against running with the early filter disabled.",
    dep: ["late-materialization", "predicate-pushdown", "column-projection"]
  },
  "encoded-predicate-evaluation": {
    s: "Evaluates filters directly on encoded data: comparing dictionary codes, skipping runs, or testing bit-packed values, so most values are never decoded.",
    d: "For a dictionary-encoded column, an equality filter on a string is converted to a test of the integer code, evaluated with vector instructions on the packed codes. For run-length encoding, one comparison covers an entire run. For frame of reference, the filter constant is shifted instead of decoding each value. The result is a bitmask over rows. This saves decode time and memory traffic, and is a main reason compressed columns can scan faster than raw ones. The difficulty is that each encoding and predicate pair needs its own path, and unsupported pairs fall back to decoding.",
    k: ["Evaluate on encoded form vs decode then filter: encoded evaluation is much faster and uses less memory, but needs a code path for each encoding and operator.", "Per-block encoding choice vs one encoding per column: per-block choice compresses better but means the filter must switch between paths from block to block."],
    p: ["A new encoding is added without a predicate path and queries on that column silently fall back to decode and become slow.", "A dictionary built per block makes code values incomparable across blocks, so the constant must be translated for each block."],
    v: "Filter an equality on a dictionary-encoded string column over a billion rows, and confirm from counters that the filter ran on codes with zero string decodes, and results match the decoded-filter path.",
    dep: ["dictionary-encoding", "run-length-encoding", "bit-packing", "simd-kernels"]
  },
  "parallel-range-scans": {
    s: "Splits the selected granule ranges into many concurrent reads across threads, files and storage devices so scan throughput approaches the speed of the hardware.",
    d: "The planned ranges are divided into tasks, each reading a run of granules from each needed column. Threads issue reads, decode and run the pipeline, with the number of reads in flight tuned to the device: many for object storage with high latency, fewer for local disks. Large requests are favored. Each thread works on a different part or range, so output order is not preserved unless requested. The difficulty is balance: too much parallelism makes random reads that slow local disks, too little leaves object storage idle waiting for responses.",
    k: ["Many small parallel reads vs few large sequential reads: parallel small reads hide latency on object storage but can thrash a local disk; large sequential reads are best for disks and waste time on slow stores.", "Preserve order vs unordered parallelism: ordered reads are required for sorted output and early stop, but limit parallelism and slow the scan."],
    p: ["Too many in-flight reads on a spinning disk turns sequential access into seeking and throughput drops.", "A single large part is assigned to one thread, leaving the rest idle."],
    v: "Scan a large table on local flash and on object storage and confirm throughput reaches a large share of device bandwidth in each case, with the number of reads in flight matching the configured depth.",
    dep: ["scan-planning", "morsel-scheduling", "read-ahead"]
  },
  "read-ahead": {
    s: "Starts reading the next blocks before the executor asks for them, and merges neighboring reads, so decode and I/O overlap and requests are large.",
    d: "As a scan advances through a column, a background task requests the following blocks into a buffer, using the known planned ranges rather than guessing from access pattern. Close ranges are coalesced into one request, even reading a small gap to avoid an extra round trip. Buffers are bounded per query. On object storage, prefetching is essential because each request has high latency. The difficulty is waste: prefetching past a limit or a cancelled query reads data nobody uses, and buffer memory multiplied by many columns and threads can be large.",
    k: ["Prefetch by planned ranges vs by observed pattern: planned ranges are exact and avoid waste, but only work when the scan plan is known; pattern-based guesses can mispredict.", "Coalesce across small gaps vs strict ranges: reading a gap saves a request and costs extra bytes, which is a good trade on high-latency storage and a poor one on local flash."],
    p: ["A query with a limit prefetches many blocks that are discarded when it finishes early.", "Buffers per column per thread add up to gigabytes on wide scans and trigger memory limits."],
    v: "Scan a table from object storage with read-ahead on and off, and confirm elapsed time drops while bytes read increase only slightly, with request count lower due to coalescing.",
    dep: ["parallel-range-scans", "object-store-cache"]
  },
  "deleted-row-filtering": {
    s: "Hides rows marked deleted or updated without rewriting the data, by reading a per-part mask or version columns and dropping those rows during the scan.",
    d: "A delete or update that avoids rewriting parts records the affected rows in a separate small structure: a bitmap of row positions for lightweight deletes, or a newer version and a tombstone flag for upserts. The scan loads the structure for each part and applies it as an extra filter, or merges versions on read. Merges later apply the changes physically and discard the marks. The difficulty is read cost: many accumulated marks slow every scan until merged, and merging across versions during reads costs more than a plain scan.",
    k: ["Mask on read vs rewrite parts immediately: masks make deletes instant and cheap but add work to every query; rewriting is expensive up front and leaves reads clean.", "Version columns resolved at read time vs resolved only by merge: read-time resolution gives current data immediately but slows queries; merge-only resolution is faster but returns stale or duplicate rows until merges finish."],
    p: ["Heavy deletes accumulate masks across thousands of parts, and every scan pays for loading them.", "A query that skips the mask step for speed returns rows that users deleted."],
    v: "Delete a million rows from a large table and confirm a count drops immediately, bytes read are unchanged until the next merge, and after merge the mask disappears and scan speed returns to baseline.",
    dep: ["mutations", "background-merges", "row-position-mapping"]
  },
  "sparse-primary-index": {
    s: "One index entry per granule of rows, holding the sort-key value of the granule's first row, small enough to stay in memory and used to find the ranges that can match a filter.",
    d: "Rows in a part are sorted by the table's sort key and divided into granules of a few thousand rows. The index stores one key tuple per granule. A filter on a prefix of the sort key is turned into a range, and a binary search over the index returns the granules to read. It does not locate single rows, since only granule boundaries are known. The index is orders of magnitude smaller than a dense one, so billions of rows need megabytes. The weakness is that only filters on the leading columns of the sort key help; others cannot use it.",
    k: ["Sparse index per granule vs dense per-row index: sparse is tiny, fast to build and cheap to maintain during merges, but only narrows to a block; dense finds single rows and costs space and write time.", "Larger vs smaller granules: larger granules shrink the index and make reads bigger, while smaller ones prune more finely and enlarge the index."],
    p: ["A filter on the second sort key column alone cannot use the index, so the whole table is scanned.", "Low-cardinality leading key columns make the index nearly useless for the high-cardinality column that queries filter on."],
    v: "Filter on the leading sort-key column and then on the third one, and confirm the first reads a tiny fraction of granules while the second reads nearly all, with the plan listing index usage for each.",
    dep: ["granules", "sort-key-selection", "sort-order-clustering"]
  },
  "min-max-zone-maps": {
    s: "The minimum and maximum of each column over each part or block, stored with the data, used to rule out blocks whose range cannot satisfy a filter.",
    d: "While a part is written, the engine records min and max per column for the whole part and, often, for each block or row group. At query time a range or equality predicate is tested against these values: if the filter value lies outside the range, the block is skipped without reading. Zone maps work best on columns correlated with the sort order, such as timestamps in time-ordered data. They cost almost nothing to store. The weakness is random data: if each block spans nearly the whole value range, no block can be excluded.",
    k: ["Block-level summaries vs part-level only: block-level summaries skip more but need more metadata to read; part-level summaries are minimal and skip only whole parts.", "Min-max vs richer summaries: min-max is trivial and general for ordered data, but cannot answer set membership, which needs Bloom filters or sets."],
    p: ["A column not correlated with sort order has every block spanning the full range, so zone maps skip nothing.", "A single outlier value inflates a block's range and prevents it from being skipped."],
    v: "Filter on a time column in a time-ordered table and confirm the profile shows only the parts and blocks overlapping the range read, then repeat on a random column and confirm nothing is skipped.",
    dep: ["granules", "file-footer-metadata"]
  },
  "bloom-filter-skipping": {
    s: "A small per-block probabilistic set of values that answers definitely absent or possibly present, letting equality filters on unsorted columns skip blocks.",
    d: "For each block, the writer adds the column's values to a bit array through several hash functions and stores it. At query time, the filter's value is hashed and tested; if any bit is zero, the block cannot contain it and is skipped. False positives cause an unnecessary read but never a wrong result. Size is set from the number of values and the false-positive rate. It helps point lookups on high-cardinality columns such as ids. The difficulty is cost-benefit: the filter takes space and write time, and gives little when values appear in most blocks.",
    k: ["Bloom filter vs exact value set per block: a Bloom filter is small and fixed in size with false positives; an exact set is precise and grows with block cardinality.", "Lower false-positive rate vs smaller size: a lower rate skips more blocks and needs more bits per value."],
    p: ["Values in the column are spread across every block, so the filter passes every block and only adds cost.", "A filter sized for the wrong block cardinality saturates and always answers possibly present."],
    v: "Look up a single id in a large table with the filter and without, and confirm the filter version reads only a few blocks where the other reads all, with results identical.",
    dep: ["skip-indexes", "granules"]
  },
  "skip-indexes": {
    s: "Optional secondary structures over blocks of rows, such as value sets, min-max on expressions or token filters for text, which let queries on non-key columns skip blocks.",
    d: "A skip index is declared on a column or expression with a granularity of several granules per index entry. During a write or merge, the engine builds its summary for each group of granules. At query time, the filter is tested against each summary to rule out groups. Types include bounded value sets for low-cardinality columns, min-max over expressions, Bloom filters and n-gram or token filters for substring search. Unlike a classic index, it never finds rows, only excludes blocks. The difficulty is effectiveness: gains depend on how well values cluster by block.",
    k: ["Add a skip index vs change the sort key: an index helps one more access path without disturbing the layout; reordering the data helps everything that correlates with the key but rewrites the table.", "Coarse vs fine granularity: coarse entries are cheap and skip little; fine entries skip more with larger metadata and more checks per query."],
    p: ["An index on a column whose values are uniformly spread over blocks never skips anything while slowing every insert and merge.", "The index is created after data exists and is not applied to old parts until a rebuild."],
    v: "Create a set index on a low-cardinality column clustered by a related key, run a filter on it, and confirm the profile shows granules dropped by the index with a matching drop in bytes read.",
    dep: ["bloom-filter-skipping", "min-max-zone-maps", "background-merges"]
  },
  "partition-pruning": {
    s: "Excludes whole partitions, usually by date, whose partition value cannot satisfy the query filter, and allows dropping or moving an entire partition cheaply.",
    d: "A partition expression, commonly the month of a date column, groups parts into partitions. The planner reads the partition key range stored for each part and eliminates those outside the filter, before any index is consulted. Partitions are also the unit for dropping, moving and detaching data. The expression should have a modest number of distinct values: too many partitions produce many small parts and slow merges and planning, since merges never cross partition boundaries.",
    k: ["Few coarse partitions vs many fine ones: coarse partitions keep part counts low and merges effective but prune less; fine partitions prune and drop precisely and produce many small parts.", "Partition for pruning vs partition for lifecycle: partitions are best chosen for data management, while the sort key provides within-table pruning."],
    p: ["Partitioning by a high-cardinality column produces thousands of partitions each with tiny parts, and planning and merging slow down.", "A query filters on a column related to but not equal to the partition expression, so no partitions are pruned."],
    v: "Query one day of a table partitioned by month and confirm only that month's partition is scanned, then drop an old partition and confirm storage drops immediately without a rewrite.",
    dep: ["data-parts", "retention-ttl"]
  },
  "sort-key-selection": {
    s: "Choosing which columns, in which order, a table is physically sorted by, the single decision that most affects scan speed, compression and which queries are fast.",
    d: "The sort key determines the order of rows inside each part. Leading columns get strong pruning from the sparse index and long runs for compression. Good choices are columns commonly filtered by equality or range, ordered from low cardinality to high so runs are longer and the prefix useful. It is fixed at table creation because changing it means rewriting data, though projections can offer alternate orders. The tension is that one order cannot serve every query: improving one access pattern worsens another.",
    k: ["Low cardinality first vs most-filtered first: low cardinality first compresses better and prunes on prefixes, while putting the most-filtered column first gives sharper pruning for that column.", "One sort key vs extra projections: one key is simple and cheap to write, while projections make other filters fast at the cost of storage and ingestion work."],
    p: ["A key chosen by guesswork leaves the most common filter outside the key prefix, so the dominant query scans the whole table.", "A timestamp placed first in the key at fine resolution makes later key columns useless for pruning and compression."],
    v: "Replay a week of real queries against two candidate keys on a sample, and compare granules read and compressed size, confirming the chosen key reads fewer granules for the top queries.",
    dep: ["sort-order-clustering", "sparse-primary-index", "projections"]
  },
  "projections": {
    s: "A hidden copy of a table's data stored in a different sort order or pre-aggregated form, kept in step with the table and chosen automatically by the planner when cheaper.",
    d: "A projection is defined by a select over the table, with its own sort order or grouping, and is stored alongside each part. When rows are inserted or merged, the projection's version of the part is built from the same rows, so it cannot drift from the base. A query whose needs are satisfied by the projection reads it instead. This gives several physical orders without changes to queries. Costs are storage and write work: each projection roughly adds the cost of another sorted copy, and merges must maintain it.",
    k: ["Projection stored per part vs separate table: per-part storage stays consistent automatically and is used transparently, while a separate table is flexible but must be kept in step by the application.", "Sorted-copy projection vs pre-aggregating projection: a sorted copy supports any query on that order, and pre-aggregation is far smaller and answers only matching group-bys."],
    p: ["Several projections multiply ingestion time and storage, and nobody notices until inserts slow.", "A projection is created after data exists and old parts lack it until materialized, so plans vary between parts."],
    v: "Create a projection sorted by a second filter column, run a query on it, and confirm the plan reads the projection with granules read dropping by orders of magnitude, with identical results to the base table.",
    dep: ["projection-routing", "data-parts", "sort-key-selection"]
  },
  "projection-routing": {
    s: "The planner step that decides whether a query can be answered from a projection, comparing estimated bytes and granules against reading the base table.",
    d: "After analysis, the planner checks, for each projection, whether its columns cover the query and its sort order or aggregation matches the filters and groupings. It estimates the data to read for each candidate with index and statistics, and picks the cheapest. Because parts may differ, some may lack the projection, so a query may read the projection for some parts and the base for others. The difficulty is matching: an expression written slightly differently from the definition, or an extra column, causes a miss, and the user never learns why the projection was not used.",
    k: ["Cost-based choice vs always use a matching projection: cost-based choice avoids slow projections on odd queries but depends on estimates; always using it is predictable and sometimes slower.", "Exact expression match vs semantic matching: exact match is simple and safe, but misses equivalent queries that semantic matching would find at higher planning cost."],
    p: ["A query uses an alias or a rewritten expression that does not match the definition, and the projection is not used.", "Mixed parts, with and without the projection, lead to a plan that reads the base table for new data and is slow."],
    v: "Explain a query that matches a projection and one that does not, and confirm the first lists the projection with fewer granules and the second reports the reason for no match.",
    dep: ["projections", "cardinality-estimation", "scan-planning"]
  },
  "materialized-views": {
    s: "A table that is filled automatically by a query over incoming inserts into a source table, so rollups and reshaped copies stay current without a scheduled job.",
    d: "A materialized view is a stored query attached to a source table. Every insert block into the source is run through the query, and the result is inserted into a target table, often one that sums or merges rows by key. Queries then read the small target. The view sees only newly inserted blocks, not the whole table, so it computes correctly only for functions that can be combined across blocks. Updates and deletes in the source are not propagated. The difficulty is correctness over time: a view defined later has no history, and a bug in it cannot fix old rows without a backfill.",
    k: ["Insert-triggered views vs periodically refreshed views: insert-triggered views are current and cheap per block, but cannot handle general queries; refresh recomputes anything and is stale between runs.", "Target table with merge-time combining vs plain append: merge-time combining keeps the target small, but queries must still re-aggregate until merges finish."],
    p: ["A view is added after the source has data and silently covers only rows inserted since.", "A deletion in the source is not reflected in the target, which then disagrees with the source."],
    v: "Create a view that sums per day, insert two batches including the same day, and confirm queries on the target return correct totals before and after merges, matching an aggregate over the source table.",
    dep: ["incremental-view-maintenance", "insert-pipeline", "pre-aggregated-tables"]
  },
  "incremental-view-maintenance": {
    s: "Updates a derived result by processing only the changed input rows and merging them into the stored state, instead of recomputing from the whole source.",
    d: "For each new batch, the view's query produces a delta, which is combined with existing state through a merge function: sums add, minima take the smaller, sketches union. Joins and distinct operations require more state or care. The state is typically held as partial aggregates that finalize at read time. Handling updates and deletes needs retractions, negative deltas that cancel old contributions. The difficulty is the class of supported queries: only those whose state is mergeable work, and consistency under replays is delicate, since a duplicated batch double-counts.",
    k: ["Maintain by deltas vs full recompute: deltas keep cost proportional to new data and need a mergeable state; recompute handles any query and costs time proportional to history.", "Support retractions vs append-only inputs: retractions allow updates and deletes in sources, but need extra state and make state larger and merges more complex."],
    p: ["A retried insert is applied twice to the view while the source deduplicated it, so totals drift apart.", "A late-arriving row for a closed window needs to update old state that the design assumed final."],
    v: "Insert a batch, replay the same batch with deduplication active, and confirm the view's totals equal a fresh recompute from the source, with the view's update cost proportional to batch size.",
    dep: ["partial-aggregation", "deduplication-on-merge", "materialized-views"]
  },
  "pre-aggregated-tables": {
    s: "Tables that store sums, counts and sketches already grouped at a coarse grain, such as per hour per customer, so dashboards read thousands of rows instead of billions.",
    d: "The engine offers table types whose merges combine rows with equal keys using aggregate states. Rows stored at hour grain are merged to the same key, and queries finalize states at read time. They are typically fed by a materialized view from a raw events table. Choosing the grain is the key decision: dimensions included determine which questions can be answered. The pitfall is that the table must be queried with the same aggregate-combining functions, and rows may not be fully merged at read time, so readers must aggregate again.",
    k: ["Coarse grain vs fine grain: coarse tables are small and fast and cannot answer finer questions; fine tables answer more and are larger.", "Store mergeable states vs finished values: states allow re-aggregating to coarser groups and distinct counts, while finished values are simpler to read and cannot be combined."],
    p: ["A query reads the table without aggregating and returns partial rows for the same key, double counting after merges lag.", "A needed dimension was left out of the grain, and the question requires going back to the raw table."],
    v: "Fill a pre-aggregated table from raw data and confirm that querying it at hour, day and month grain yields results equal to aggregating the raw table directly, with reads touching orders of magnitude fewer rows.",
    dep: ["materialized-views", "partial-aggregation", "background-merges"]
  },
  "insert-pipeline": {
    s: "Turns an incoming batch of rows into a sorted, encoded, compressed data part and registers it atomically, so readers see all of the batch or none.",
    d: "Rows are parsed, validated against the schema, split by partition, sorted by the sort key, split into columns, encoded, compressed and written as files in a new part directory. Indexes and statistics are built along the way, and any materialized views fire on the same block. The part is then renamed or committed to the catalog in one step. Insert speed depends on batch size: each batch becomes a part, so many small inserts create many small parts. The hard part is throughput and the trade-off between latency and the part count.",
    k: ["Large batches vs frequent small inserts: large batches create few efficient parts and add latency; small inserts are visible sooner and create part storms that merges struggle to clear.", "Sort at insert vs at merge: sorting at insert gives immediately queryable sorted parts and costs CPU on the write path; deferring would speed inserts and slow reads."],
    p: ["Thousands of single-row inserts per second create more parts than merges can reduce, and the table starts rejecting writes.", "A partial failure leaves a half-written part that must be invisible and cleaned later."],
    v: "Insert a million-row batch and confirm it appears as one new part with correct row count, is visible atomically to concurrent readers, and after a crash mid-insert no partial rows are visible.",
    dep: ["data-parts", "encoding-selection", "general-codecs", "materialized-views"]
  },
  "async-insert-batching": {
    s: "Buffers many small inserts in memory on the server and flushes them together as one part, so clients can send rows freely without creating thousands of tiny parts.",
    d: "Small inserts are accepted into a server-side buffer, grouped by table and format, and flushed when the buffer reaches a size or age threshold. The client is acknowledged either after the flush, which keeps durability, or immediately, which is faster and loses buffered data in a crash. Merging inserts from many clients produces large parts. Latency before data is visible equals the flush interval. The hard part is the choice of acknowledgment and handling of duplicates when a client retries an unacknowledged insert.",
    k: ["Acknowledge after flush vs immediately: after-flush acknowledgment is durable and slower per request; immediate acknowledgment is fast and loses data on a crash.", "Server-side batching vs client-side batching: server-side lets simple clients write freely and takes memory on the server; client batching gives control and requires each producer to implement it."],
    p: ["Immediate acknowledgment is used for important data, and a crash loses the buffered seconds without the clients knowing.", "A client retry after a timeout inserts the same rows twice because the first flush had in fact succeeded."],
    v: "Send ten thousand single-row inserts from many clients, confirm only a few parts are created, all rows appear after the flush interval, and killing the server before acknowledgment shows exactly which rows were lost.",
    dep: ["insert-pipeline", "deduplication-on-merge"]
  },
  "data-parts": {
    s: "The unit of storage: an immutable directory of column files, indexes and metadata for a batch of sorted rows, created by inserts and replaced by merges.",
    d: "Each part holds a sorted run of rows split into column files, plus the sparse index, checksums, row counts, min-max values, partition information and a record of which columns it contains. Parts are never modified: new data creates new parts, and merges produce a larger part and then retire the originals. A table is the set of its active parts, and queries pin the set they read. The immutability simplifies concurrency and replication, and makes backups easy: parts are hard-linkable. The cost is the need to merge continuously and the overhead of many small parts.",
    k: ["Immutable parts vs in-place updates: immutability gives lock-free reads, simple snapshots and replication of whole files, at the cost of merges and write amplification.", "Compact format for small parts vs wide format for large: one file for tiny parts avoids many small files, and one file per column wins when parts are large and skipping matters."],
    p: ["A table accumulates tens of thousands of small parts after a load burst and queries slow down on planning and file opening.", "A part deleted while a long query is reading it must be kept alive until the query finishes."],
    v: "List a table's active parts, run a merge, and confirm the new part's row count equals the sum of the originals, old parts are retired only after concurrent queries finish, and checksums validate on read.",
    dep: ["column-files", "granules", "file-footer-metadata"]
  },
  "background-merges": {
    s: "Continuous background work that combines several sorted parts into one larger sorted part, reducing the part count and applying deduplication, expiry and aggregation.",
    d: "A merge reads the chosen parts column by column, merges rows by sort key using a heap or a k-way merge, applies the table's merge-time logic, then writes the new part and swaps it in. Merges run in a bounded pool, are throttled against foreground I/O and are cancelled if a part disappears. Larger merges produce better layout and take longer. The difficulty is competition: merges consume disk bandwidth and CPU that queries also need, and when inserts outpace merging, the part count rises until the engine slows or rejects inserts.",
    k: ["Merge aggressively vs lazily: aggressive merging keeps queries fast and uses more I/O continuously; lazy merging saves resources and leaves many parts that slow reads.", "Merge in the background vs on demand at query time: background merging pays the cost off the read path, while merging at query time returns correct but slow answers."],
    p: ["A merge of very large parts holds the disk for hours and starves smaller merges, so the part count climbs.", "Disk space shortage blocks a merge, since it needs space for the old and new parts together."],
    v: "Insert fifty small parts, wait for the merge pool, and confirm the system table shows the merge with its input parts and output size, the part count drops, and queries return identical results throughout.",
    dep: ["data-parts", "merge-policy", "memory-accounting"]
  },
  "merge-policy": {
    s: "The rules that choose which parts to merge and when, balancing the number of parts against write amplification and the cost of rewriting old, large data.",
    d: "A typical policy picks adjacent parts of similar size within a partition, preferring merges where the total is under a size limit and the ratio of sizes is balanced, which keeps a logarithmic number of size tiers. Each row is rewritten about as many times as there are tiers. Policies consider free disk space, the maximum merged part size and the time since the last merge. The trade-off is write amplification against read amplification: more merging means better reads and more writes.",
    k: ["Size-tiered merges vs leveled merges: tiered merges write less and leave more overlapping parts; leveled merges keep fewer overlaps and rewrite data more.", "Cap the maximum part size vs allow unbounded merges: a cap prevents giant merges that block the pool and leaves a few large parts; no cap gives the best layout and risks very long merges."],
    p: ["The size cap stops merging of a partition that then retains hundreds of medium parts permanently.", "A change in insert pattern leaves a policy tuned for large batches unable to cope with streams of tiny ones."],
    v: "Insert at a steady rate for an hour and confirm the part count stabilizes at a bounded number per partition, with the amount of data rewritten per inserted byte matching the expected number of tiers.",
    dep: ["background-merges", "partition-pruning"]
  },
  "deduplication-on-merge": {
    s: "Collapses rows that share a key into one during merges, keeping the latest version, which turns an append-only store into one that supports upserts eventually.",
    d: "A table with a replacing merge rule keeps, for each key, the row with the highest version or the last inserted. The collapse happens when parts holding the same key are merged, so duplicates are present until then. Queries that need exact results must either force merge-like resolution at read time, which costs more, or accept duplicates. Insert-level deduplication also exists: identical blocks inserted twice are detected by checksum and ignored, which makes retries safe. The difficulty is eventual behavior: counts and sums may include duplicates for an unpredictable time.",
    k: ["Merge-time deduplication vs read-time resolution: merge-time is cheap on reads and stale until merged; read-time resolution is exact and slows every scan.", "Block checksum deduplication vs key-based deduplication: checksums make retried batches safe at low cost and miss the same row in a different batch; key-based collapses real updates and needs a version."],
    p: ["A dashboard sums a table with unmerged duplicate versions and double counts recent updates.", "A retry with a slightly different batch composition evades the checksum check and inserts duplicates."],
    v: "Insert a key twice with increasing versions, query before merge and see two rows, query with read-time resolution and see one, force a merge and confirm a single row remains with the newer version.",
    dep: ["background-merges", "mutations", "sort-order-clustering"]
  },
  "mutations": {
    s: "Updates and deletes applied to existing data by rewriting the affected parts in the background, or by lightweight marking, since parts are immutable.",
    d: "A mutation is recorded as a command with a predicate and new values, and is applied to each existing part: parts containing matching rows are rewritten with the changes, others are left alone or hard-linked. Mutations run as an ordered queue, one version at a time, and complete when all parts have been processed. Lightweight alternatives write a mask or patch part and defer rewriting. Because every rewritten column and part costs I/O, analytical engines discourage frequent small updates. The difficulty is visibility and cost: a mutation is not instant and a wide table rewrite may take hours.",
    k: ["Rewrite parts vs lightweight mask or patch: rewriting leaves clean fast reads and costs heavy I/O; masks apply instantly and cost every read until merged.", "Update only the changed columns vs the whole row: touching only the changed column files rewrites little data and needs the columnar layout and positional alignment."],
    p: ["A routine update of a few rows rewrites hundreds of gigabytes because each row sits in a different part.", "Mutations queued behind a long one are not applied for hours, and users assume they failed."],
    v: "Update one column of ten rows scattered across parts, confirm only the touched column files are rewritten, queries see the new values after completion, and the mutations table shows each part as done.",
    dep: ["data-parts", "background-merges", "deleted-row-filtering"]
  },
  "schema-evolution": {
    s: "Adding, dropping, renaming and retyping columns on a table of immutable parts, usually without rewriting existing data.",
    d: "Adding a column records it in table metadata; old parts lack its file, and readers fill in the default value on the fly, so the change is instant. Dropping a column hides it and deletes its files lazily or at the next merge. Renames change the mapping from name to file. Type changes need either lazy conversion on read or a rewrite of the affected column. Because each column is a separate file, these are far cheaper than in a row store. The difficulty is consistency: replicas and in-flight queries must agree on the schema version, and old parts differ from new.",
    k: ["Lazy default on read vs backfill by rewrite: lazy defaults make adding a column instant, and every read must handle parts without the file; backfilling costs I/O and leaves uniform parts.", "Metadata-only rename vs file rename: metadata-only is instant and file names diverge from column names; renaming files keeps them aligned and needs care for concurrent readers."],
    p: ["A retyped column is read through a conversion for old parts, slowing queries until a rewrite is done.", "A replica applies the schema change later than others and returns different columns for the same query."],
    v: "Add a column with a default to a table of a billion rows, confirm it completes in seconds, a query on the column returns the default for old parts and new values for new data, and the file count of old parts is unchanged.",
    dep: ["data-parts", "column-files", "cluster-metadata"]
  },
  "stream-ingestion": {
    s: "Continuously consumes records from a message log or stream and writes them in batches, tracking offsets so delivery is at-least-once or effectively exactly-once.",
    d: "A consumer reads from partitions of a log, buffers records for a time or size window, and inserts each batch through the normal pipeline. Offsets are committed after the insert succeeds, so a crash causes re-reading and duplicates unless the batch is idempotent: the same offsets produce the same block, which block-level deduplication then rejects. Parallelism follows the partitions of the source. The challenge is the trade-off between latency and efficiency, since small batches deliver fresh data and create many parts, and lag when the source outpaces ingestion.",
    k: ["Commit offsets after insert vs before: after gives at-least-once and duplicates on crash; before risks losing data and cannot produce duplicates.", "Fixed batch boundaries tied to offsets vs time-based batches: offset-based batches are replay-identical, which allows deduplication, and time-based batches are simpler but produce different blocks on retry."],
    p: ["A slow insert makes consumer lag grow without alarm until retention in the source deletes unread records.", "A poison record fails parsing and blocks the whole partition's progress."],
    v: "Produce a known set of ten thousand records, kill the consumer mid-batch, restart it, and confirm the table holds each record exactly once and committed offsets equal the last inserted offset.",
    dep: ["insert-pipeline", "async-insert-batching", "deduplication-on-merge"]
  },
  "distribution-key": {
    s: "The expression used to assign rows to shards, deciding data balance, whether joins can be local, and how many shards each query must contact.",
    d: "Each row's key is hashed or mapped to a shard, so rows with equal keys land together. A key such as the customer id keeps one customer's data on one shard, so per-customer queries touch one node and joins on that key are co-located. A random key balances perfectly and forces every query to contact every shard. The key can generally not be changed without redistributing data. The difficulty is skew and access: a hot key concentrates load on one shard, and choosing for balance can defeat locality.",
    k: ["Hash of a business key vs random: a business key allows shard-local queries and joins and can skew; random gives perfect balance and gives every query full fan-out.", "Fixed shard count vs consistent hashing with virtual shards: a fixed count is simple and expensive to resize, and virtual shards allow smoother growth with more metadata."],
    p: ["One very large customer's rows all sit on a single shard, which becomes the limit of every query involving them.", "Changing the shard count requires reshuffling nearly all data."],
    v: "Load a table with skewed keys and confirm per-shard row counts, then run a single-key query and check from the plan that exactly one shard is contacted.",
    dep: ["cluster-metadata", "rebalancing"]
  },
  "query-fanout": {
    s: "The coordinator sends each shard a sub-query, streams back partial results and merges them into the final answer, tolerating slow or failed shards by retrying on replicas.",
    d: "A query received by any node becomes the coordinator. It prunes shards using the distribution key if possible, rewrites the query for each shard, picks a replica for each, and starts the sub-queries in parallel. Shards send back partial aggregates or filtered rows, which the coordinator merges. If a shard fails or times out, the coordinator can retry another replica or, optionally, return a partial result. The challenge is stragglers: the query finishes only when the slowest shard does, so tail latency grows with shard count.",
    k: ["Retry on another replica vs fail the query: retrying hides failures and doubles work for slow shards, while failing is predictable and brittle.", "Allow partial results when a shard is down vs require completeness: partial results keep dashboards alive and can mislead, completeness is correct and fails more often."],
    p: ["One slow shard sets the latency of every query, and adding shards makes the tail worse.", "A coordinator receiving all results from thousands of shards becomes a network bottleneck."],
    v: "Run an aggregate across all shards, take one replica offline mid-query, and confirm the query finishes using another replica with the correct total and the system table recording the retry.",
    dep: ["distributed-planner", "replica-selection", "cluster-metadata"]
  },
  "replication-log": {
    s: "An ordered log of changes to the part set, such as new parts, merges and mutations, which each replica replays to reach the same set of parts.",
    d: "On an insert, the receiving replica writes the part locally and records the event in a shared, ordered log. Other replicas read the log and fetch the part's files from a peer, or perform the same merge or mutation locally. Replicas converge on the same parts, although at different times, and merges are assigned to one replica and copied by the rest or done in parallel with the same result. The log lives in the metadata service. The difficulty is lag and coordination load: replicas can fall behind, and every part, including tiny ones, produces log entries.",
    k: ["Replicate files (parts) vs replicate row operations: file replication reuses the already sorted and compressed result and moves bytes; operation replication moves less and repeats computation on every replica.", "Each replica merges independently vs one merges and others fetch: independent merges save network and risk byte-different results; a single merger gives identical parts and a single point of work."],
    p: ["A replica falls behind after a burst of small inserts because the log and fetches are per part.", "A lost entry in the log leaves a replica permanently missing a part until a repair check runs."],
    v: "Insert on one replica, confirm the same part count and row total appear on the others within seconds, then pause a replica, insert more, resume, and confirm it catches up to identical checksums.",
    dep: ["cluster-metadata", "data-parts", "background-merges"]
  },
  "replica-selection": {
    s: "Chooses which replica serves each shard's part of a query, by load, locality, freshness and error history, spreading reads and avoiding lagging copies.",
    d: "Strategies include random, nearest by network distance, fewest in-flight queries, or in-order with failover. The selection can exclude replicas whose lag exceeds a threshold, since they would return stale data, and can send the same sub-query to two replicas and take the first answer to cut tail latency. Replicas for one shard may also split the scan by parts, so a single query uses every copy. The difficulty is consistency: choosing a lagging replica returns older data, which can make repeated queries appear to go backward in time.",
    k: ["Load balance across replicas vs prefer one replica for cache locality: balancing evens out load and cold caches on each, while sticking to one keeps its caches hot and risks overload.", "Hedged duplicate requests vs single requests: hedging cuts tail latency and spends extra capacity; single requests are cheaper and wait for the slow node."],
    p: ["A lagging replica is chosen and a dashboard shows numbers lower than a minute ago.", "A replica that is up but slow keeps being chosen because health checks only test liveness."],
    v: "Pause replication on one replica, run a freshness-sensitive query repeatedly with a lag limit, and confirm it never uses that replica and the system table shows selection reasons.",
    dep: ["replication-log", "query-fanout"]
  },
  "cluster-metadata": {
    s: "A small, strongly consistent coordination service that stores the replication log, table schemas, shard maps and which replica holds which part.",
    d: "Metadata that must agree across nodes lives in a consensus-based store: table definitions, the list of parts and their replicas, leader assignments for merges, and replication queues. Nodes watch it for changes. The data volume is small compared with the tables, but its access rate is high, since each part and merge produces writes. It is the main availability dependency: if it is unreachable, replicated tables become read-only, and data nodes keep serving reads from what they have. The difficulty is load and sizing, since it must handle the part churn.",
    k: ["External consensus service vs built-in consensus in the data nodes: an external service is mature and separate to operate, while built-in consensus reduces dependencies and ties metadata to data nodes.", "Store the part list centrally vs let replicas discover parts from each other: central storage gives one authoritative view and a load point; discovery spreads load and complicates consistency."],
    p: ["A burst of tiny inserts overloads the coordination store, and all replicated tables become read-only.", "The service's data directory fills up with old log entries because cleanup is not configured."],
    v: "Stop the coordination service while queries run, and confirm reads continue and inserts to replicated tables fail with a clear error, then restart it and confirm writes and replicas resume without data loss.",
    dep: []
  },
  "rebalancing": {
    s: "Moves data between shards when nodes are added or removed or when load is uneven, copying parts to new owners while queries continue.",
    d: "A rebalancing plan assigns ranges or parts to new locations. Because parts are immutable files, a part can be copied to the target, verified by checksum, registered there, and only then removed from the source, with queries resolving through the shard map at each point. Some systems rebalance by whole partitions, others by hash ranges. Because rows are assigned by the distribution key, moving rows between shards can mean rewriting parts rather than copying them. The difficulty is the cost: it takes network and disk away from queries, and during it the data exists in two places.",
    k: ["Move whole parts or partitions vs rewrite by key range: moving whole units is cheap and requires the key to map to partitions; rewriting by key is flexible and costs a full re-sort and re-encode.", "Throttled background movement vs fast copy: throttling protects queries and takes days for big clusters; fast copy finishes sooner and hurts latency."],
    p: ["A query runs during a move and reads some rows twice, once from the source and once from the target.", "Adding a node does nothing for existing data because nothing triggers a rebalance, and new data alone fills it."],
    v: "Add a node to a three-node cluster, run rebalancing, and confirm row counts per node converge, total rows are unchanged, queries return identical results throughout, and old copies are removed after validation.",
    dep: ["distribution-key", "cluster-metadata", "data-parts", "replication-log"]
  },
  "tiered-storage": {
    s: "Keeps recent data on fast local disks and older parts on cheaper object storage, moving them by age or size, with queries reading from either transparently.",
    d: "Storage policies define volumes in order from fast to slow, and rules move whole parts when the fast volume fills or when parts exceed an age. Parts on object storage hold column files as objects, with metadata and indexes often kept locally so planning does not need remote reads. Some designs keep one shared copy of data in object storage with only caches on nodes. Reads from the cold tier are slower, so caches and read-ahead are essential. The difficulty is the unseen cost: a query that touches old data on the cold tier can be a hundred times slower than the same one on hot data.",
    k: ["Local disks plus a cold tier vs object storage as the primary copy: tiers keep hot reads fast and manage two systems; object storage as primary simplifies scaling and replication and makes caching essential.", "Move by age vs by space: age rules are predictable for time series and ignore access patterns; space rules react to pressure and can move data still being queried."],
    p: ["A dashboard query without a time filter reads cold objects for the whole history and takes minutes.", "Metadata kept only remotely makes planning slow because it needs many small requests."],
    v: "Set a rule to move parts older than a week, confirm the system table shows those parts on the cold volume, a query on them returns the same results with higher but bounded latency, and local disk use falls.",
    dep: ["object-store-cache", "data-parts", "retention-ttl"]
  },
  "failure-detection": {
    s: "Detects dead or slow nodes through heartbeats and session timeouts and triggers re-routing of queries and repair of missing replicas.",
    d: "Nodes register ephemeral entries in the coordination service and refresh them periodically; an expired entry marks the node as gone. Queries then avoid it, and replicas that should have a part but lost it, or whose copy is corrupt, are repaired by fetching from healthy peers after checking checksums. Failures of a disk are handled by marking the volume unusable and refetching parts. The difficulty is distinguishing slow from dead: too short a timeout causes flapping and repeated repairs, too long leaves queries hitting a broken node.",
    k: ["Short timeouts vs long timeouts: short timeouts detect failure fast and cause false positives in busy periods; long timeouts are stable and slow to react.", "Automatic repair vs operator-approved repair: automatic repair restores redundancy quickly and can amplify load or copy bad data; manual repair is slower and safe."],
    p: ["A node under heavy merge load misses heartbeats, is declared dead and triggers a mass of repairs that increase the load.", "A corrupt part is detected only when a query reads it, long after it was written."],
    v: "Kill a node and confirm within the timeout it is excluded from query routing, queries succeed from replicas, and after restart it is repaired to identical part checksums as its peers.",
    dep: ["cluster-metadata", "replication-log", "replica-selection"]
  },
  "column-files": {
    s: "One file, or one object, per column per part, holding that column's values in row order, so reading a column never reads others.",
    d: "Within a part, each column is stored as a data file of compressed blocks and a companion mark file locating each granule's start in both compressed and decompressed space. Values appear in the part's sort order, and every column of the part has the same number of rows, so position i in one file corresponds to position i in all. Small parts may pack all columns into one file with offsets. The layout makes column reads sequential and adding columns cheap. The difficulty is the file count: wide tables with many parts create enormous numbers of files.",
    k: ["File per column vs packed columns in one file: per-column files make projection and column changes trivial and multiply file counts; a packed file reduces count and needs offset tables.", "Marks with compressed and uncompressed offsets vs block-aligned granules: two-level offsets allow granules smaller than a compression block, and aligned granules simplify seeking but lock sizes together."],
    p: ["A table with thousands of columns and tens of thousands of parts exhausts file descriptors and slows opening.", "Corruption of one column file makes the part unreadable only for queries touching that column, which hides the problem."],
    v: "Write a part with five columns, list its files, and confirm each column file plus its marks exist, a query on one column opens only that file, and all columns decode to the same row count.",
    dep: ["granules", "general-codecs", "row-position-mapping"]
  },
  "granules": {
    s: "The smallest unit of reading and skipping: a run of consecutive rows, typically eight thousand, whose index entry and column data are addressed together.",
    d: "A part's rows are cut into granules of a fixed row count or byte target. Each column has a mark for each granule pointing into its file, and the sparse index has one entry per granule. A scan reads whole granules, never part of one, and compression blocks are aligned with or contain granules. Granule size trades pruning precision against metadata size and read efficiency. Adaptive granularity cuts granules by bytes so wide rows get fewer rows per granule and keep reads of similar size.",
    k: ["Fixed row count vs adaptive by bytes: fixed counts are simple and make positions arithmetic, but wide rows make huge granules; adaptive keeps read size even and needs offset tables per column.", "Small vs large granules: small granules prune precisely and enlarge index and marks; large ones read more irrelevant rows and shrink metadata."],
    p: ["Very wide rows with a fixed granule row count produce granules of hundreds of megabytes that read slowly and defeat pruning.", "A point lookup reads a full granule of thousands of rows to return one."],
    v: "Count granules in a part against rows divided by the granule size, filter for one row, and confirm exactly one granule per needed column was read, with bytes equal to that granule's compressed size.",
    dep: ["column-files", "file-footer-metadata"]
  },
  "file-footer-metadata": {
    s: "A section in each file or part describing its structure: schema, row counts, block offsets, per-block statistics, encodings and checksums, read first to plan and verify reads.",
    d: "Self-describing formats keep a footer with the schema, the row groups with their byte ranges, encodings per column chunk and min-max statistics. A reader fetches the tail of the file, parses it, and then issues targeted reads for just the needed chunks. Checksums enable detecting corruption. For object storage, the footer being at the end lets one ranged read fetch it. The difficulty is size and fetch cost: for files with thousands of columns, the footer is large and every query must read it, and repeated reads are why metadata caches exist.",
    k: ["Footer at the end of the file vs header at the start: a footer is written once after streaming the data and needs a seek or ranged read to find; a header needs sizes known in advance.", "Rich statistics per chunk vs minimal: richer statistics skip more and enlarge the footer, minimal footers are cheap and prune less."],
    p: ["A truncated upload loses the footer and the entire file is unreadable even though the data is there.", "A footer written by an older version lacks statistics a newer planner assumes and produces wrong pruning."],
    v: "Open a file, read only its footer, and confirm schema, row count and per-chunk offsets let you decode one column chunk and match the original values, with a checksum mismatch detected on a flipped byte.",
    dep: ["column-files", "granules"]
  },
  "validity-bitmaps": {
    s: "A bit per value recording whether it is null, stored separately from the values, so null handling is a mask operation and the value array stays dense.",
    d: "A nullable column stores its values contiguously with a bitmap, one bit per row, or a byte mask in simpler designs. Operators combine masks with vector logic: the output validity of an addition is the AND of the inputs' validity. Columns declared non-null skip the bitmap entirely and save space and time. Sparse columns that are mostly null may store only the non-null values with their positions. The difficulty is semantics: three-valued logic, aggregates that skip nulls and joins that never match nulls must all follow the same rules.",
    k: ["Separate validity bitmap vs sentinel value in the data: bitmaps are exact and cost a bit per row; sentinels cost nothing and make a legitimate value impossible.", "Nullable by default vs not null by default: nullable by default matches SQL expectations and adds overhead everywhere; not null by default saves space and requires explicit declaration."],
    p: ["A column made nullable out of habit adds a bitmap read and mask to every scan with no nulls present.", "An operator forgets to combine validity and treats the placeholder under a null as a real value."],
    v: "Store a column with nulls and apply sum, count, equality and a join, and confirm results follow SQL null semantics, with the count of non-null values equal to the bitmap's set bits.",
    dep: ["column-files"]
  },
  "variable-length-data": {
    s: "Storage for strings and binary values as an array of offsets plus one concatenated byte buffer, so lengths are fixed-width and values are contiguous.",
    d: "Instead of storing each string with a length prefix, the column keeps an offsets array, with entry i giving the end of value i in a shared bytes buffer. Fixed-width offsets allow random access and vectorized length computation. Short strings and long text compress very differently, so dictionary or prefix encodings are applied depending on repetition. Very large values are kept in separate files or overflow areas. The difficulty is size and cache behavior: a single huge value inflates a block, and string comparisons cost far more than integer ones.",
    k: ["Offsets plus byte buffer vs length-prefixed values: offsets give random access and simple vector loops; length prefixes append in one pass and need scanning to find a value.", "Inline long values vs spill to separate storage: inline keeps reads simple and bloats blocks with rarely read data; separation keeps blocks compact and adds a lookup."],
    p: ["One multi-megabyte value in a block makes every query on the column read and decompress it.", "A comparison operator treats strings as fixed-width and truncates, so long values compare equal."],
    v: "Store strings of widely varying length including empty and very long ones, read back every value by position, and confirm exact equality with the originals and that offsets equal cumulative lengths.",
    dep: ["column-files", "string-compression"]
  },
  "nested-columns": {
    s: "Arrays, maps and structs stored by flattening each leaf field into its own column with extra data that records where each value belongs to its parent record.",
    d: "A struct becomes one column per field. An array adds an offsets column for lengths and stores the elements in one flat column; a map becomes two parallel arrays. Some formats instead store levels that encode nesting depth and presence. A query reading one sub-field reads only that field's files and the offsets it needs. The benefit is the same skipping as for flat columns. The difficulty is the amount of bookkeeping: reconstructing whole records is expensive, and filters over array elements need functions that apply across offsets.",
    k: ["Offsets columns vs repetition and definition levels: offsets are simple and give random access to arrays; levels encode arbitrary nesting compactly and are harder to decode quickly.", "Flatten dynamic JSON into subcolumns vs store as text: subcolumns give column-speed reads and compression for common keys; text is flexible and parses on each query."],
    p: ["Selecting a whole nested record reads dozens of files and reassembles it more slowly than a row store would.", "A semi-structured column with tens of thousands of distinct keys creates a file explosion."],
    v: "Store records with an array of structs, read one sub-field, and confirm only that field's files and offsets are read, with element counts equal to the sum of array lengths.",
    dep: ["column-files", "validity-bitmaps", "variable-length-data"]
  },
  "row-position-mapping": {
    s: "The rule that ties values in different column files together: row i in every column of a part is the same row, so positions identify rows without stored row ids.",
    d: "No column stores a key for its values. Instead, all columns in a part have identical row order, and a row is identified by its offset in the part. Late materialization, deletion masks, projections of filters and joins that return positions all use these offsets, often combined with a part id. This saves space and makes assembling a row a set of positional lookups. The cost is rigidity: reordering rows in one column is impossible, and any rewrite of a part must rewrite all columns that depend on order.",
    k: ["Implicit positions vs explicit row ids: implicit positions cost no storage and break when rows are reordered; explicit ids survive reordering and cost space.", "Part-local positions vs global ids: local positions are compact and need the part to stay stable; global ids survive merges and require remapping on every merge."],
    p: ["A merge changes row positions and a stored position list from an earlier step becomes invalid.", "A column added later is filled in the wrong order for old parts if it is backfilled from a differently sorted source."],
    v: "Take a part, read the same row position across three columns, and confirm they belong to the same logical record as checked against the original input, including after a deletion mask is applied.",
    dep: ["column-files", "granules"]
  },
  "sort-order-clustering": {
    s: "Rows within each part are stored sorted by the sort key, so equal and nearby values sit together, which makes pruning, compression and merge joins effective.",
    d: "Each part is internally sorted when written, and merges preserve order. Parts overlap in key ranges, since each came from a different batch, so a query reads all parts but finds the matching range inside each by index. Over time merging reduces overlap within partitions. Sorted data compresses far better, since sorted columns have long runs and small deltas, and the leading column is cheapest of all. The effect is strongest for the first key columns and weaker for later ones.",
    k: ["Sort every part vs leave insert order: sorting costs CPU at insert and gives pruning and compression; unsorted append is faster to write and loses both advantages.", "Global order across parts vs order within each part: global order allows ranges to be read once and needs expensive rewriting; per-part order is cheap and requires reading from every part."],
    p: ["Heavy out-of-order arrival makes every part cover the entire key range, so no part can be skipped by min-max.", "Sorting on a high-cardinality leading column destroys runs in later columns and compression falls sharply."],
    v: "Write the same data sorted and unsorted, compare compressed sizes of each column and the granules read by a key filter, and confirm the sorted version is smaller and reads far less.",
    dep: ["sort-key-selection", "data-parts"]
  },
  "dictionary-encoding": {
    s: "Replaces each distinct value with a small integer code and stores the dictionary once, ideal for strings and other columns with few distinct values.",
    d: "The writer collects distinct values in a block or part, assigns codes, and stores a dictionary and the array of codes, which are then bit-packed or run-length encoded. Queries can compare, group and join on codes, decoding only for output. Dictionaries can be per block, giving small codes but different mappings per block, or global, which makes codes comparable across blocks. When distinct values exceed a limit, the writer abandons the dictionary. The difficulty is cardinality: a high-cardinality dictionary is as large as the data.",
    k: ["Per-block dictionary vs global dictionary: per-block is simple and gives small codes with no coordination; global makes codes comparable and needs maintenance as values arrive.", "Fall back to plain values above a cardinality threshold vs always encode: falling back avoids a dictionary larger than the data, and encoding always is simpler and wasteful for unique values."],
    p: ["A nearly unique string column is dictionary-encoded and the dictionary roughly doubles its size.", "Codes from two blocks are compared without translating through each dictionary."],
    v: "Encode a ten-million-row column of fifty distinct strings, confirm stored size falls by an order of magnitude, equality filters run on codes, and decoded output equals the input.",
    dep: ["bit-packing", "encoding-selection"]
  },
  "run-length-encoding": {
    s: "Stores a repeated value once with its repeat count, very compact for sorted or low-cardinality columns where equal values are adjacent.",
    d: "Consecutive equal values collapse into pairs of value and run length. A sorted column of a hundred thousand distinct values over a billion rows becomes a hundred thousand runs. Operators can work run by run: a filter tests the value once for a whole run and a sum multiplies the value by the length. It combines with dictionary encoding by compressing the code array. The difficulty is that it helps only with adjacent repeats, so it works for leading sort-key columns and does the opposite on random data.",
    k: ["Run-length on sorted columns vs bit-packing: run-length wins hugely on long runs and loses on short ones, where bit-packing gives steady compression.", "Process by runs vs expand then process: processing by runs makes some operators sublinear in rows, and every operator must be written to handle runs."],
    p: ["Short runs make the encoded form larger than the raw data because every run carries a count.", "An operator expands runs to full arrays and erases the advantage."],
    v: "Encode a sorted column with long runs and a shuffled copy of it, and confirm the sorted copy shrinks by orders of magnitude and a count with a filter touches only run entries.",
    dep: ["sort-order-clustering", "encoding-selection"]
  },
  "delta-encoding": {
    s: "Stores differences between neighboring values, or between each value and a block base, so slowly changing numbers such as timestamps and ids need only a few bits each.",
    d: "Delta encoding stores the first value and then each value minus its predecessor; a second-order delta stores the difference of differences, which makes regularly spaced timestamps nearly zero. Frame of reference subtracts a block minimum so all values fit in a small number of bits. The deltas are then bit-packed or compressed with a general codec. Decoding is a running sum, which resists vectorization, so frame of reference is often preferred for speed. The difficulty is random access: reading value i needs earlier values unless blocks are short.",
    k: ["Delta vs frame of reference: delta gives the smallest size for monotonic series and needs sequential decoding; frame of reference decodes values independently and compresses less.", "Short delta blocks vs long ones: short blocks allow random access and cost a base value per block; long blocks compress more and make seeks slow."],
    p: ["Applying delta to random values widens the range and makes encoding larger than the original.", "A single outlier in a block widens the bit width for every value in it."],
    v: "Encode a regular timestamp column with delta of delta and confirm size is near one bit per value, then decode and compare equal to the original, including at block boundaries.",
    dep: ["bit-packing", "encoding-selection"]
  },
  "bit-packing": {
    s: "Stores integers using only as many bits as the largest value in the block needs, packed tightly, which decodes fast with vector instructions.",
    d: "For a block of values, the writer finds the bit width required for the maximum and writes each value in that many bits, back to back. Decoders unpack many values per instruction by shifting and masking. Packing underlies dictionary codes, delta values and run lengths. Widths from one to sixty-four bits need generated variants. Filters can be evaluated on packed data directly. The difficulty is outliers: one large value forces the entire block to a wide width, so some designs store outliers separately as exceptions.",
    k: ["One width per block vs patched exceptions for outliers: one width is simple and fast; exceptions keep widths small despite outliers and add a second structure to decode.", "Smaller blocks for adaptive width vs larger blocks: smaller blocks adapt to local value ranges and carry more headers; larger blocks amortize headers and are hurt by outliers."],
    p: ["A single large value in each block makes packing no better than raw integers.", "Unpacking code for rarely used widths is untested and returns wrong values only for those widths."],
    v: "Pack random integers for every bit width from one to sixty-four, unpack, and confirm values are equal, with size close to count times width and decode throughput in billions of values per second.",
    dep: ["simd-kernels"]
  },
  "float-compression": {
    s: "Compresses floating-point columns by predicting each value from the previous one and storing only the XOR or difference bits that change, which suits sensor and metric series.",
    d: "Adjacent measurements are often similar, so XOR-ing a value with its predecessor leaves long runs of zero bits that can be stored in a few bits of header and the differing middle part. Other methods split the bytes of each value into separate streams so that sign, exponent and high mantissa bytes compress well together. Prices or measurements with few decimal places can be converted to scaled integers. Lossy options reduce precision on request. The difficulty is that noisy floats are close to incompressible, and decoding is sequential.",
    k: ["Lossless XOR scheme vs byte-splitting with a general codec: XOR achieves high compression for smooth series and decodes sequentially; byte splitting is simpler and vectorizes and compresses less.", "Convert decimals to scaled integers vs store as binary floats: integers compress better and are exact for money; floats handle any value and cannot represent decimals exactly."],
    p: ["Random noise columns see no gain and pay the sequential decode cost.", "Scaled integer conversion silently overflows for large values."],
    v: "Compress a smooth sensor series and a random series, and confirm the smooth series shrinks several fold and both round-trip bit for bit, including negative zero, infinities and NaN payloads.",
    dep: ["general-codecs", "encoding-selection"]
  },
  "string-compression": {
    s: "Specialized compression for text columns: prefix sharing for sorted strings, small static symbol tables for short strings, and dictionary encoding for repeated values.",
    d: "Sorted strings often share long prefixes, so each is stored as the length of the prefix shared with its predecessor plus the suffix. Symbol-table methods replace frequent short substrings with one-byte codes learned from a sample and support fast random access and comparison on compressed strings. Repeated values go to a dictionary. General codecs are applied on top. Some formats keep a small summary per block for fast pruning on string ranges. The difficulty is varied content: urls, logs and free text compress differently, and decoding costs matter for substring searches.",
    k: ["Symbol tables with random access vs general codec on blocks: symbol tables let filters and comparisons run on compressed data and compress somewhat less; block codecs compress more and need full decompression.", "Prefix compression vs plain storage: prefix compression pays off for sorted keys and breaks random access within a block."],
    p: ["A symbol table trained on early data compresses later data with a different pattern poorly.", "A search for a substring requires decoding every string even though the column is compressed."],
    v: "Compress a column of urls and one of random text, and confirm sizes fall by the expected ratio for the url column, all strings round-trip exactly, and equality filters on compressed data return correct rows.",
    dep: ["dictionary-encoding", "general-codecs", "variable-length-data"]
  },
  "general-codecs": {
    s: "Byte-oriented compression such as fast block codecs and slower, stronger ones, applied after encoding and chosen per column to balance size and CPU.",
    d: "After lightweight encoding, each block of a column file is passed through a general codec. Fast codecs decompress at several gigabytes per second per core and give modest ratios, and heavy codecs compress much more and cost more CPU to write and read. Codec choice can differ by column and by tier: fast for hot data, strong for cold. Block size affects ratio and seek cost. Checksums are stored per block. The difficulty is choosing: a heavy codec on a hot column makes queries CPU-bound, while a light codec on cold data wastes storage.",
    k: ["Fast codec vs high-ratio codec: fast codecs keep scans I/O-bound and use more storage; high-ratio codecs save space and shift the bottleneck to CPU.", "Per-column codec choice vs a single default: per-column choice uses each column's characteristics and requires understanding the data; a default is simple and mediocre."],
    p: ["A strong codec is applied to every column and scans become CPU-bound on a fast disk.", "Compressing already compressed or encrypted data gains nothing and burns CPU."],
    v: "Write the same column with each codec, and compare compressed size, write time and scan throughput, confirming the chosen codec meets the target ratio without scan throughput falling below the I/O rate.",
    dep: ["encoding-selection", "column-files"]
  },
  "encoding-selection": {
    s: "The step that picks the encoding and codec for each column chunk when data is written, by sampling or trying candidates and keeping the smallest or fastest.",
    d: "Given a block, the writer samples values, computes cardinality, run counts, value range and sortedness, and picks a dictionary, run-length, delta or plain encoding, followed by a general codec. Some engines try several encodings on a sample and take the best, others follow fixed rules per type. Choices may be stored per block so a column can change as data does. Declared hints in the schema override defaults. The difficulty is cost: trying all options slows ingestion, and a choice good for size can be bad for scan speed.",
    k: ["Choose automatically per block vs fix at schema design: automatic choice adapts to the data and costs write time and unpredictability; fixed choices are predictable and go stale.", "Optimize for size vs for scan speed: smaller encodings reduce I/O and can increase decode work, and speed-oriented ones are bigger and quicker to scan."],
    p: ["Sampling the first rows picks an encoding that is wrong for the rest of the block.", "Different blocks of one column use different encodings, and an operator handles only the common one efficiently."],
    v: "Write a column with changing characteristics, inspect the stored encoding per block, and confirm each block's choice matches its statistics and every block round-trips exactly.",
    dep: ["dictionary-encoding", "run-length-encoding", "delta-encoding", "bit-packing"]
  },
  "block-cache": {
    s: "A memory cache of decompressed column blocks, so repeated scans of hot data skip both disk reads and decompression.",
    d: "Blocks are cached by file and offset, with a size-bounded eviction policy. The cache sits above the operating system page cache, which holds compressed bytes. Because analytical scans read large ranges once, a plain least-recently-used policy lets one big scan evict everything useful, so engines use scan-resistant policies or bypass the cache for large reads. Decompressed data costs more memory than compressed. The difficulty is deciding what is worth caching: a cache that holds the wrong data uses memory the query execution needs.",
    k: ["Cache decompressed blocks vs rely on the page cache for compressed bytes: decompressed caching saves CPU and uses several times more memory; the page cache is automatic and holds more data.", "Scan-resistant eviction vs plain recency: scan resistance protects hot data from one big scan and is more complex; plain recency is simple and easily flushed."],
    p: ["A single full-table scan evicts the working set of dashboards, and every following query is cold.", "The cache takes memory needed by aggregations and causes spilling."],
    v: "Run a hot dashboard query repeatedly, confirm hit rate near one hundred percent and latency drop, then run a full scan and confirm the dashboard's blocks remain cached.",
    dep: ["memory-accounting", "column-files"]
  },
  "index-metadata-cache": {
    s: "Keeps sparse indexes, marks, file footers and part lists in memory, so planning a query does not need disk or object store reads.",
    d: "Sparse index and mark files are small relative to data but must be consulted for every query, so they are loaded at startup or on first use and held in memory. File footers and per-column statistics are cached the same way. The cache is bounded and evicts the least useful entries. On a cold start, loading metadata for millions of parts can take minutes, during which queries are slow. The difficulty is memory: metadata scales with the number of parts and columns, not with bytes of data, so a table with many tiny parts costs a lot.",
    k: ["Load all metadata at startup vs lazily on demand: eager loading gives predictable query latency and delays startup and uses memory; lazy loading starts fast and makes first queries slow.", "Unbounded in-memory indexes vs size-limited cache: unbounded is fast and can exhaust memory with many parts; bounded protects memory and makes some queries reload."],
    p: ["A restart makes the first queries on each table slow while metadata loads.", "Metadata for a table with a million parts uses more memory than expected and crowds out data caches."],
    v: "Restart a node, run a query and note planning time cold, run it again and confirm planning time falls to milliseconds, with hit counters rising and memory use within the configured bound.",
    dep: ["sparse-primary-index", "file-footer-metadata", "memory-accounting"]
  },
  "result-cache": {
    s: "Stores the final result of a query keyed by its text and settings, so identical repeated queries, as dashboards issue, return instantly while the underlying data is unchanged.",
    d: "After a query finishes, if its result is small and it took long enough, the result is stored with a time to live. A later identical query from a user allowed to see the same rows returns it without execution. Entries are invalidated by expiry or by changes to the source tables. Non-deterministic functions, such as the current time, make queries uncacheable. Row policies and settings are part of the key. The difficulty is staleness and security: serving a cached result to a different role must not leak data, and a result may be out of date by up to the time to live.",
    k: ["Time-to-live expiry vs invalidate on change: expiry is simple and allows staleness up to the limit; invalidation is exact and requires tracking dependencies and is costly on busy tables.", "Per-user cache keys vs shared across users: per-user keys are safe and give fewer hits; shared keys are efficient and require identical policies to be safe."],
    p: ["A dashboard shows data minutes old and nobody realizes because the result was cached.", "A result computed under one role's row policy is served to another because the key omitted the policy."],
    v: "Run a slow query twice, confirm the second returns in milliseconds from cache, insert new data, and confirm the third run is recomputed when invalidation is on, with both roles' results correct under policies.",
    dep: ["row-policies", "access-control", "memory-accounting"]
  },
  "object-store-cache": {
    s: "A local disk cache of objects or byte ranges fetched from object storage, so repeated reads of the same data avoid slow, costly remote requests.",
    d: "When the cold tier is object storage, each node keeps recently read column chunks and metadata on local flash, keyed by object and range, and checks it before issuing a remote read. It is filled on demand or pre-warmed, with eviction by recency and space limit. Because it holds ranges, not whole files, it caches just the columns that queries use. On a shared-storage design, every node keeps its own cache, and queries are routed to the node holding the data. The difficulty is warm-up and consistency: a new node starts with a cold cache, and objects can be replaced.",
    k: ["Cache ranges vs whole objects: ranges hold just what queries read and need more bookkeeping; whole objects are simple and fetch unneeded columns.", "Route queries to the node with the warm cache vs spread evenly: routing raises hit rates and creates hotspots; even spread balances load and warms every cache."],
    p: ["A new or restarted node serves its first queries entirely from remote storage, with high latency.", "An object replaced under the same name is served stale from cache."],
    v: "Run a scan on cold storage twice and confirm remote requests and latency fall on the second run, local cache usage rises by the bytes read, and bytes fetched match the touched column ranges.",
    dep: ["tiered-storage", "read-ahead", "memory-accounting"]
  },
  "memory-accounting": {
    s: "Tracks every allocation by query, operator and background task, enforces limits at each level and kills or spills when a limit would be exceeded.",
    d: "Each allocation of significant size is charged to a hierarchy: the server, a user, a query and an operator. The tracker compares the running total to limits at each level, and operators can be asked to spill, or the query is failed, when it would be breached. Background merges and caches are accounted separately. Allocations are counted in the allocator, not by each operator's own estimate, so it catches hidden usage. The difficulty is overhead and fairness: per-allocation tracking adds cost, so small changes are batched, and one query can still use most of the server's share before limits trigger.",
    k: ["Hard per-query limit vs shared pool with overcommit: hard limits protect others and fail legitimate large queries; overcommit uses memory efficiently and risks server-wide exhaustion.", "Account in the allocator vs in each operator: allocator-level accounting is complete and adds overhead on hot paths; operator-level accounting is cheap and misses untracked structures."],
    p: ["Memory held by caches and merges is not counted against queries, and the server runs out while every query is within its limit.", "A limit per query on a hundred-node cluster allows a hundred times the intended total."],
    v: "Run queries that allocate more than their limit, and confirm each fails or spills at the limit, the reported peak matches resident memory growth within a small tolerance, and the server total stays below its cap.",
    dep: []
  },
  "sort-order-design": {
    s: "The decision about how rows are ordered inside every part, which determines pruning, compression, merge behavior and which queries are fast, affecting every layer from planner to storage.",
    d: "Because the order of rows is fixed at write time, the sort key reaches everywhere. The planner uses it for range pruning and early termination, the scan for the sparse index, the encodings for long runs and small deltas, the merge process for combining parts and the joins for merge strategies. Distribution adds a second key at the shard level. The decision is hard to reverse, so choices follow the dominant access patterns and are tested against real queries. Alternate orders come from projections at the price of extra storage and write work.",
    k: ["Optimize for the dominant query vs balance many: tuning for the top query makes it extremely fast and others slow; balancing gives uniform mediocre performance.", "Fix the order early vs change it later by rewriting: early design avoids costly rewrites and relies on predicting the workload; later change fits reality and rewrites terabytes."],
    p: ["A workload changes and the sort key no longer matches any common filter, and the table needs a full rewrite.", "Different teams assume different orders, and queries are written that cannot use the index."],
    v: "Compare the granules read, bytes scanned and compressed size of the top twenty production queries for the chosen key against alternatives on a sample, and confirm the chosen key is best or near best for the dominant ones.",
    dep: ["sort-key-selection", "sparse-primary-index", "sort-order-clustering", "run-length-encoding"]
  },
  "immutable-parts": {
    s: "Stored data is never modified in place: inserts write new parts, merges write new parts, and old ones are removed afterward, which simplifies reads, replication, backup and recovery.",
    d: "Because a part, once written and checksummed, never changes, readers need no locks, a query's view is the set of parts at its start, and replicas copy whole files. Updates, deletes, schema changes and expiry are handled by creating new parts or marks, then retiring old ones when no reader uses them. Backups are hard links to the files. The costs are write amplification through merges, delayed visibility of changes in compact forms and the need to track part lifetimes. Every layer assumes it, from the scan to the replication log.",
    k: ["Immutable files vs in-place update: immutability gives lock-free reads, cheap snapshots and simple replication, and costs merges and slow updates; in-place update is flexible and needs locking and logging.", "Delete old parts immediately vs reference-counted retirement: immediate deletion saves space and breaks running queries; reference counting is safe and delays space reclamation."],
    p: ["A long-running query pins old parts and disk fills with data that merges already replaced.", "Code that assumes a part can be edited slightly, such as patching a value, breaks replicas and backups."],
    v: "Hold a long query open while a merge finishes, and confirm the query completes correctly from the old parts, and the old files are deleted only after it ends.",
    dep: ["data-parts", "background-merges", "replication-log", "mutations"]
  },
  "consistency-model": {
    s: "What readers are promised about freshness and agreement: snapshot reads of a fixed part set, eventual convergence of replicas, and the delay between an insert and visibility everywhere.",
    d: "A single query reads a consistent set of parts on each shard, but is not generally a single point-in-time snapshot across shards. An insert is visible on its receiving replica at once and on others after replication. Merge-time deduplication and view updates add eventual behavior. Settings let a query require acknowledgment from several replicas or avoid lagging ones. Most analytical engines do not offer multi-statement transactions, and some inserts into many tables are not atomic together. These choices shape every component that touches visibility.",
    k: ["Eventual replica visibility vs quorum on write: eventual is fast and means different replicas may disagree briefly; quorum waits for replicas and reduces risk.", "Per-shard snapshots vs global snapshot: per-shard is cheap and may mix times across shards; a global snapshot needs coordination that analytical systems avoid."],
    p: ["A user inserts and immediately queries via another replica and does not see their own data.", "A report joins two tables inserted separately and sees one updated and the other not."],
    v: "Insert a batch with quorum acknowledgment and immediately read from every replica, confirming visibility everywhere, then repeat without quorum and measure the replication delay before all replicas agree.",
    dep: ["replication-log", "replica-selection", "deduplication-on-merge", "materialized-views"]
  },
  "memory-budget": {
    s: "How limited memory is divided among query execution, caches, merges, buffers and metadata, so no consumer starves the others and large queries degrade gracefully by spilling.",
    d: "The server has a total budget split among competing uses: per-query working memory, the block and metadata caches, merge buffers, insert buffers and the operating system's page cache. Each consumer has a limit, and some can shrink on demand: caches evict and operators spill. Allocation decisions in planners, operators and storage all consult the same accounting. Mistakes appear in surprising places, since the cost of a join or group-by is memory proportional to the data, which is the main reason an analytical query fails.",
    k: ["Static partition of memory vs dynamic sharing: static partitions are predictable and leave unused memory; dynamic sharing uses everything and lets bursts starve other consumers.", "Fail large queries vs spill them: failing is fast and protects the server; spilling lets them finish and takes far longer and uses disk."],
    p: ["Caches are sized to use all memory and nothing remains for aggregation, causing constant spilling.", "Merge buffers grow with column count and compete with queries at peak ingestion."],
    v: "Run a mixed workload with full caches, large aggregations and active merges, and confirm total resident memory stays below the limit, no process is killed and queries either finish or fail with a clear memory error.",
    dep: ["memory-accounting", "spill-to-disk", "block-cache", "background-merges"]
  },
  "observability": {
    s: "The ability to see what the system is doing and why a query was slow: query logs, per-operator profiles, part and merge tables, queue depths and replication lag, all queryable.",
    d: "Every layer reports into the same system tables: planning time, rows and bytes read, granules skipped, memory peaks, cache hits, merge activity, replication queue length and node health. Together they answer the usual questions: why was this query slow, why are inserts rejected, which tables have too many parts, which replica is behind. The data is itself columnar and cheap to aggregate. Operators build alerts on a few measures: part count, merge backlog, replication delay, memory pressure and query tail latency.",
    k: ["Log every query vs sample: full logs allow tracing any query and grow large; sampling is cheap and can miss the one that mattered.", "Collect inside the engine vs external agents: engine tables are complete and queryable, and fail with the engine; external agents survive failures and see less detail."],
    p: ["Alerts are set on averages and miss the tail latency that users feel.", "Logs of the failing node are lost because the log table lived on it."],
    v: "Pick a slow query from yesterday and use only system tables to determine its plan, bytes read, granules skipped, wait time in queue and memory peak, then explain its latency without rerunning it.",
    dep: ["system-metrics-tables", "query-profiler", "query-audit-log"]
  },
  "fault-tolerance": {
    s: "How the system absorbs failures: replicas for each shard, retries of sub-queries, verification by checksum, repair from peers and atomic part commits that leave no partial data.",
    d: "Disk and node failures are expected. Each shard has replicas that hold identical parts, queries fail over to another replica, and a lost replica is rebuilt by copying parts from peers. Atomic commits guarantee a crashed insert or merge leaves no visible partial part. Checksums catch silent corruption on read and during background scrubbing. Object storage offers durability at the tier level. What is not tolerated is loss of the coordination service for writes or loss of all replicas of a shard, so backups of parts or object storage copies matter.",
    k: ["More replicas vs lower cost: more replicas improve availability and read capacity, and multiply storage and write work; fewer are cheaper and riskier.", "Failover on error vs fail fast: failover keeps queries running and may mask a degrading node; failing fast surfaces problems and interrupts users."],
    p: ["Replicas share a failure domain such as one rack, and a single failure takes all copies.", "Corruption replicates before it is detected because checksums are verified only on read."],
    v: "Kill one replica during a heavy query and an insert, and confirm the query completes, the insert is visible exactly once, and after replacement the node rebuilds identical parts verified by checksum.",
    dep: ["replication-log", "failure-detection", "query-fanout", "data-parts"]
  }
});
